WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.491
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:08.460
Welcome to the MIT Center for
Art, Science and Technology

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:12.780
2021 Symposium, Unfolding
Intelligence, the Art

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:16.530
and Science of
Contemporary Computation.

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:19.110
CAS was established
in 2012 with the goal

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:21.660
of building and
building on connections

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:24.300
between the worlds of art,
science, and technology.

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:26.400
This is the third in
a series of symposia

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.020
the CAS has convened since then.

00:01:28.020 --> 00:01:31.050
And as with its predecessors,
we bring together

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:35.490
artists, scientists, engineers,
and humanists from within MIT

00:01:35.490 --> 00:01:37.830
and from the world
at large to discuss

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:39.810
areas of rapidly
evolving research

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:43.650
and urgent social relevance,
and define in that dialogue

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:47.650
stimulation, confirmation,
provocation, intersection,

00:01:47.650 --> 00:01:51.550
and we hope common purpose.

00:01:51.550 --> 00:01:54.880
At MIT, CAS partners with
departments, labs, and centers

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:57.280
to integrate the arts
across the curriculum

00:01:57.280 --> 00:02:00.790
to enrich and encourage artistic
collaboration, and to provide

00:02:00.790 --> 00:02:03.940
support to faculty and
members of the MIT community

00:02:03.940 --> 00:02:06.160
as they pursue their
own artistic practice

00:02:06.160 --> 00:02:09.080
and/or research.

00:02:09.080 --> 00:02:11.140
In addition to
symposia like this,

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:13.690
CAS facilitates the sharing
of this creative work

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:16.210
beyond the Institute
by producing concerts,

00:02:16.210 --> 00:02:18.820
exhibitions, and
publications, and making

00:02:18.820 --> 00:02:21.470
them available to the public.

00:02:21.470 --> 00:02:24.140
So thank you for
being with us today.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:26.140
We hope you will join
us throughout the week

00:02:26.140 --> 00:02:29.500
at virtual events addressing
the aesthetic, technical,

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.830
and critical issues pertaining
to artificial intelligence

00:02:32.830 --> 00:02:35.170
and computational media.

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:37.910
We also look forward to
seeing you on Friday,

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:40.570
April 9 as the
symposium culminates

00:02:40.570 --> 00:02:44.140
with a live interactive event
to which all attendees are

00:02:44.140 --> 00:02:47.470
invited, and which you can
join presenters and artists

00:02:47.470 --> 00:02:51.010
in breakout rooms to explore
hidden threads between all that

00:02:51.010 --> 00:02:52.630
has been discussed this week.

00:03:08.630 --> 00:03:12.100
Welcome to our
panel on bias and AI

00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:21.580
for exploring all of the kind of
issues where we can understand

00:03:21.580 --> 00:03:26.260
how algorithms have been
leveraged for a number

00:03:26.260 --> 00:03:29.920
of technological advances,
at the same time as encoding

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:33.070
a number of types of
discriminatory practices

00:03:33.070 --> 00:03:34.090
these days.

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:37.570
Terms like bias, and
AI, and algorithmic bias

00:03:37.570 --> 00:03:40.460
have nearly entered the
common parlance these days.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:42.590
But it wasn't always the case.

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:47.500
Even though many
technologies in the past

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:50.160
have also implemented bias.

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:53.120
So for example, we
could think back

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.650
towards collaborative filtering
algorithms, commonly used

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:00.380
and commonly
benefited from today,

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:02.810
whether it's from
recommendations for purchases,

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:05.200
or songs, and so forth.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:08.290
At the same time, when you look
at how collaborative filtering

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:10.780
algorithms work, these
algorithms that are frequently

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:13.360
used in recommender
systems, you can

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:16.930
say that they build consensus
in a particular way that

00:04:16.930 --> 00:04:18.610
can encode bias.

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:21.399
That is you can think about
the collaborative filtering

00:04:21.399 --> 00:04:25.030
algorithm as a very, as working
on a very sparse matrix.

00:04:25.030 --> 00:04:28.540
That is, if you imagine
a kind of a system

00:04:28.540 --> 00:04:31.000
where you have all of
the users of the system

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:34.150
along a vertical axis
and all of the items

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:36.670
that can be recommended
along the horizontal axis,

00:04:36.670 --> 00:04:37.720
you're going to know--

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:40.030
then the system is going
to try to figure out

00:04:40.030 --> 00:04:43.810
for each cell would
that person want

00:04:43.810 --> 00:04:47.840
to purchase, or understand,
or use that particular item?

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:49.570
So you can see that
most of those cells

00:04:49.570 --> 00:04:51.790
are going to be filled,
and the algorithm is going

00:04:51.790 --> 00:04:53.440
to have to try to fill it.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:57.070
It would do so by trying
to find networks of users,

00:04:57.070 --> 00:05:01.360
like minded users, and build
consensus, show those items

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:02.560
to those users more.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:05.110
And then you have this
escalating process

00:05:05.110 --> 00:05:08.320
of consensus building,
in which case

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:10.420
all the individuals
will tend to converge

00:05:10.420 --> 00:05:12.070
towards similar preferences.

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:15.340
In fact, in the early literature
around collaborate filtering

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:18.880
algorithms, people who didn't
fit neatly into the rubric

00:05:18.880 --> 00:05:22.690
were called in early
papers either black sheep

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:25.300
or white sheep, tellingly.

00:05:25.300 --> 00:05:27.580
Fields such as computer
support co-operative work

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:29.800
within computer
science have long

00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:32.200
looked at issues like
the sociotechnical gap,

00:05:32.200 --> 00:05:34.570
trying to understand
why systems often

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:37.930
fail for social reasons rather
than technological reasons.

00:05:37.930 --> 00:05:41.020
Thinkers such as Phil
[? Agery ?] and Terri Winograd

00:05:41.020 --> 00:05:43.510
have been critics of AI.

00:05:43.510 --> 00:05:45.760
And Fill in
particular suggesting

00:05:45.760 --> 00:05:47.980
this idea of critical
technical practices

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:50.110
where we consider
their foundations as we

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:52.630
think about how we
engineer systems.

00:05:52.630 --> 00:05:55.420
And then sociology of science,
we have people like Bruno

00:05:55.420 --> 00:05:57.370
[? Latour ?] who
would look at how

00:05:57.370 --> 00:06:02.500
people and systems all within
the same network exchange

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:05.070
values with one another.

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:10.160
Now the term bias and
AI has proliferated

00:06:10.160 --> 00:06:14.270
in part because of the use of
artificial neural networks.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:16.100
These used to be--

00:06:16.100 --> 00:06:18.710
used-- 20 and 30
years ago primarily

00:06:18.710 --> 00:06:21.290
either for particular classes
of engineering problems

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:23.810
or in cases where
computer scientists

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:26.540
wanted enhanced
biological plausibility.

00:06:26.540 --> 00:06:30.260
However, 20 years ago, they
weren't in as widespread use

00:06:30.260 --> 00:06:35.180
as today because such
systems were criticized

00:06:35.180 --> 00:06:39.620
for a variety of reasons, such
as biological plausibility

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:42.990
as we learned that it's not
just whether a neuron fires,

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:44.960
but the rate of fire,
and much more that

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:48.440
determines its operation
within the human brain.

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:50.720
But now with the exception
of deep learning,

00:06:50.720 --> 00:06:53.090
the classes of
problems have expanded

00:06:53.090 --> 00:06:57.290
immensely that artificial
neural networks can approach.

00:06:57.290 --> 00:06:59.090
And because of
this proliferation

00:06:59.090 --> 00:07:01.500
of using artificial
neural networks,

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:04.790
the inclusion with the
enhanced media attention

00:07:04.790 --> 00:07:09.590
around particular kind
of social issues of bias,

00:07:09.590 --> 00:07:16.190
then this topic of bias and
AI has become more well known.

00:07:16.190 --> 00:07:19.910
As Jeff [INAUDIBLE] and
[? Lee ?] Starr have put it,

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:25.250
we can combine our technical
and scientific approaches

00:07:25.250 --> 00:07:28.520
with the wisdom of race critical
and feminist scholarship

00:07:28.520 --> 00:07:30.920
in order to more
completely understand

00:07:30.920 --> 00:07:34.550
the kind of operation
of these systems.

00:07:34.550 --> 00:07:38.480
Hence, the panel
from today is aimed

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:41.270
to allow us to benefit from
the insights of computer

00:07:41.270 --> 00:07:45.650
science, sociology, reflective
technologies from the arts,

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:47.540
all to gain different
perspectives

00:07:47.540 --> 00:07:51.320
on the issue of bias and AI.

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:54.890
And I want to finally just
mention one other point related

00:07:54.890 --> 00:08:00.110
to bias and AI before I begin
to introduce our panelists.

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:02.240
It's that we can
turn the lens around.

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:05.390
So I've been really pleased
in some of our own work

00:08:05.390 --> 00:08:08.360
to use deep learning or
statistical techniques

00:08:08.360 --> 00:08:12.110
such as archetypal
analysis to try

00:08:12.110 --> 00:08:17.450
to understand why it's widely
used systems such as avatars,

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:19.550
and games, and social
media, social media

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:20.900
profiles, and more.

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:26.060
And expose the kind of
biases and that they

00:08:26.060 --> 00:08:28.580
tend to implement, such
as not being optimized

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:30.930
for particular
categories of people

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:36.190
as regards to race or
gender in particular.

00:08:36.190 --> 00:08:38.740
So to begin with, I
mentioned benefiting

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:42.669
from the wisdom of sociology,
computer science, and the arts.

00:08:42.669 --> 00:08:44.920
We have first of
all Ruha Benjamin

00:08:44.920 --> 00:08:47.200
who specializes in
interdisciplinary study

00:08:47.200 --> 00:08:50.990
of science, medicine,
technology, race, ethnicity,

00:08:50.990 --> 00:08:52.890
gender, and knowledge and power.

00:08:52.890 --> 00:08:54.670
She's the author
of People Science:

00:08:54.670 --> 00:08:58.300
Bodies and Rights on
the stem cell frontier

00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:01.140
and race after technology.

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:05.670
And she is a professor
at Duke University.

00:09:05.670 --> 00:09:09.270
She has written numerous
articles and book chapters,

00:09:09.270 --> 00:09:11.820
been awarded fellowships
and grants from the National

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:16.140
Science Foundation, Ford
Foundation, and much more.

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:18.370
In computer science and
information science,

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:20.160
we have Jon Kleinberg.

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:22.620
His research focuses on
interaction of algorithms

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:25.650
and networks, and the roles
they play in large scale

00:09:25.650 --> 00:09:27.730
social information systems.

00:09:27.730 --> 00:09:31.590
His work has been supported
by NSF Career Award, ONR Young

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:35.040
Investigator Award, MacArthur
Fellowship, Sloan Foundation

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:36.870
Fellowship, and
much more, as well

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:39.700
as grants from
Facebook, Google, Yahoo,

00:09:39.700 --> 00:09:42.600
but MacArthur
Foundation and the NSF.

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:45.630
And many other organizations.

00:09:45.630 --> 00:09:49.800
And in terms of reflecting
on these kind of systems

00:09:49.800 --> 00:09:53.280
that we have Behnaz Farahi
here who is an award winning

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:56.970
designer, critical maker
based in Los Angeles

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:59.730
who holds a PhD in
interdisciplinary and media

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:03.790
arts and practice from USC
School of Cinematic Arts.

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:07.090
She explores how to foster
an empathetic relationship

00:10:07.090 --> 00:10:11.830
between the human body
and space and space

00:10:11.830 --> 00:10:13.750
around it using
computational systems,

00:10:13.750 --> 00:10:17.560
addressing issues ranging from
emotion to social interaction.

00:10:17.560 --> 00:10:19.960
And she uses fabrication
techniques along

00:10:19.960 --> 00:10:21.755
with computational
design in her work.

00:10:21.755 --> 00:10:23.380
Her work is in the
permanent collection

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:26.740
the Museum of Science
and Industry in Chicago.

00:10:26.740 --> 00:10:28.240
She's been exhibited
internationally

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:30.400
in Arts Electronica and more.

00:10:30.400 --> 00:10:34.060
And featured across the popular
press, ranging from BBC,

00:10:34.060 --> 00:10:39.610
to The Guardian, Wired, and
many other journalistic venues.

00:10:39.610 --> 00:10:48.400
And finally, she is also a
recipient of many fellowships

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:52.110
too, such as the Rock
Hudson Fellowship.

00:10:52.110 --> 00:10:54.870
So I'm very, very
pleased to have

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:58.450
these distinguished
panelists with us here today.

00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:01.650
First off is going
to be Ruha Benjamin.

00:11:01.650 --> 00:11:05.850
You will see a recorded
video of her speaking.

00:11:05.850 --> 00:11:10.560
And afterwards, we'll return for
a few live verifying questions

00:11:10.560 --> 00:11:12.900
before continuing on
with our other panelists

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:14.490
in a similar format.

00:11:14.490 --> 00:11:19.330
And then going to a broad
panel discussion and Q&amp;A.

00:11:19.330 --> 00:11:24.620
So without further ado, I'd
like to hand it off and begin

00:11:24.620 --> 00:11:26.590
Ruha's presentation.

00:11:26.590 --> 00:11:28.620
Thank you so much
for the invitation

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:31.050
to be part of this conversation.

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:35.670
When it comes to our theme,
unfolding intelligence,

00:11:35.670 --> 00:11:39.480
I want to suggest that to
talk about intelligence

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:42.240
is to talk about eugenics.

00:11:42.240 --> 00:11:46.770
For many people, the E word
conjures a particular place--

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:48.510
Nazi, Germany.

00:11:48.510 --> 00:11:50.640
Forgetting that
the Nazis learned

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:53.250
from US based
scientists and doctors,

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:56.310
and that Hitler called
Madison Grant's book,

00:11:56.310 --> 00:12:00.270
The Passing of the
Great Race his Bible.

00:12:00.270 --> 00:12:04.710
The word eugenics also
conjures a specific time period

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:10.080
that we have supposedly passed,
when in fact, eugenic logics

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:15.060
and imaginaries continue
to infect so many areas

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:17.430
of our contemporary lives.

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:20.910
From the way patients
are treated and triaged,

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:25.500
to the way that schoolchildren
are ranked and rated.

00:12:25.500 --> 00:12:28.230
In short, the
eugenics period never

00:12:28.230 --> 00:12:31.080
ended, but rather unfolded.

00:12:31.080 --> 00:12:35.130
Mercurial in the way that it
has taken on different forms

00:12:35.130 --> 00:12:40.140
and functions, including
in our present age of AI.

00:12:40.140 --> 00:12:42.390
For our conversation
today, I want

00:12:42.390 --> 00:12:46.650
to briefly highlight the
entanglement between eugenics

00:12:46.650 --> 00:12:48.510
and statistics.

00:12:48.510 --> 00:12:51.990
And since we're still in the
midst of the spring semester,

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:54.360
I'd like to assign
some homework,

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:58.170
and encourage everyone to read
this article authored by Aubrey

00:12:58.170 --> 00:12:59.910
Clayton a few months ago.

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:01.710
Published a few months ago.

00:13:01.710 --> 00:13:05.640
In it she writes that
ideally, statisticians

00:13:05.640 --> 00:13:09.540
would like to divorce these
tools from the lives and times

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:12.090
of the people who created them.

00:13:12.090 --> 00:13:14.700
It would be convenient
if statistics

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:18.720
existed outside of history,
but that's not the case.

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:22.800
Statistics as a lens through
which scientists investigate

00:13:22.800 --> 00:13:26.160
real world questions
has always been

00:13:26.160 --> 00:13:30.960
smudged by the fingerprints of
the people holding the lens.

00:13:30.960 --> 00:13:34.380
Statistical thinking
and eugenicists thinking

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:37.530
are in fact deeply intertwined.

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:39.720
And many of the
theoretical problems

00:13:39.720 --> 00:13:43.020
with methods like significance
testing first developed

00:13:43.020 --> 00:13:46.170
to identify racial
differences are

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:51.420
remnants of their original
purpose to support eugenics.

00:13:51.420 --> 00:13:57.510
In other words, eugenics haunts
any discussion of intelligence.

00:13:57.510 --> 00:14:02.730
So if statistical thinking
will not save us, what will?

00:14:02.730 --> 00:14:04.890
Black feminist
thought, of course.

00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:08.550
And here I draw upon the
wisdom of Toni Cade Bambara

00:14:08.550 --> 00:14:13.140
when she writes, "Not
all speed is movement."

00:14:13.140 --> 00:14:15.420
Again, this idea
could be applied

00:14:15.420 --> 00:14:18.090
into so many different
areas of our lives.

00:14:18.090 --> 00:14:21.480
But here I want us to think
about it in the context

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:23.760
of technological development.

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:30.000
Which too often is conflated,
falsely, with social progress.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.680
Technological development can
actually reinforce and sediment

00:14:34.680 --> 00:14:36.000
social inequalities.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:38.910
In fact, its default
settings should have it

00:14:38.910 --> 00:14:41.280
that we shouldn't be
surprised when it does.

00:14:41.280 --> 00:14:45.840
And this idea is
easier to translate

00:14:45.840 --> 00:14:48.120
and to shape our
critical thinking

00:14:48.120 --> 00:14:50.600
when it comes to the past.

00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:53.820
It's easier to recognize
when technology and data have

00:14:53.820 --> 00:14:57.160
been used to harm
different groups,

00:14:57.160 --> 00:15:01.500
whether it's the Deadly
Data that IBM helped

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:04.590
in supporting the Holocaust
in terms of punch cards

00:15:04.590 --> 00:15:06.810
to surveil and track
populations that

00:15:06.810 --> 00:15:09.870
were eventually exterminated.

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:13.690
But it's harder, I think, to
apply this to our present,

00:15:13.690 --> 00:15:17.250
especially when it comes to
what we might call Do-Gooding

00:15:17.250 --> 00:15:20.430
Data where the
framing and the motive

00:15:20.430 --> 00:15:24.150
is to purportedly
help different groups.

00:15:24.150 --> 00:15:27.330
In this case, a quick
example comes to us

00:15:27.330 --> 00:15:30.210
from Minnesota where the
public school district there

00:15:30.210 --> 00:15:32.610
and the police
department joined forces

00:15:32.610 --> 00:15:36.570
under the umbrella of what they
call the Innovation Project.

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:39.480
And this was a
project that intended

00:15:39.480 --> 00:15:42.990
to collect and centralize
data from a number

00:15:42.990 --> 00:15:45.660
of different local
agencies in order

00:15:45.660 --> 00:15:50.670
to help predict the riskiness
of young people, their risk

00:15:50.670 --> 00:15:53.880
of having future encounters
with law enforcement.

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:56.850
The do-gooding aspect
was that these agencies

00:15:56.850 --> 00:15:59.010
hoped to intervene early.

00:15:59.010 --> 00:16:02.490
But ultimately, the community
rallied around and put an end

00:16:02.490 --> 00:16:05.250
to this innovation project,
pointing to the fact

00:16:05.250 --> 00:16:08.310
that the vast majority
of these agencies

00:16:08.310 --> 00:16:09.990
actually did not
have a good track

00:16:09.990 --> 00:16:12.720
record when it
came to supporting

00:16:12.720 --> 00:16:14.510
the well-being of young people.

00:16:14.510 --> 00:16:16.260
They pointed in
particular to the data

00:16:16.260 --> 00:16:20.070
that would be used as the
input for this project.

00:16:20.070 --> 00:16:22.260
The different
attendance records,

00:16:22.260 --> 00:16:25.410
whether family members
had been involved

00:16:25.410 --> 00:16:26.940
with the criminal
justice system,

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:28.720
Child Protective Services.

00:16:28.720 --> 00:16:30.780
So all of the
input data actually

00:16:30.780 --> 00:16:33.330
were drawn from agencies
and institutions that

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:37.410
have routinely harmed, surveil,
and mistreat young people.

00:16:37.410 --> 00:16:40.560
So why should the community
expect a different outcome,

00:16:40.560 --> 00:16:42.160
a good outcome?

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:43.680
And so again, it's
harder to think

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:48.390
about this critically in light
of the questions of eugenics

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:51.390
and how this supports the
ranking and the rating

00:16:51.390 --> 00:16:53.350
of different groups.

00:16:53.350 --> 00:16:55.950
And so as a starting
point to think critically

00:16:55.950 --> 00:16:59.100
about our present moment,
our do-gooding efforts,

00:16:59.100 --> 00:17:02.790
I want to suggest that when it
comes to computational depth,

00:17:02.790 --> 00:17:05.700
deep learning, machine
learning, without

00:17:05.700 --> 00:17:09.210
historical and sociological
depth, what we're actually

00:17:09.210 --> 00:17:12.329
producing is
superficial learning.

00:17:12.329 --> 00:17:16.650
We really have to situate our
questions, and our designs,

00:17:16.650 --> 00:17:21.250
and our goals in a much deeper,
historical, and social context

00:17:21.250 --> 00:17:25.859
so that we don't unwittingly
reproduce racial inequalities

00:17:25.859 --> 00:17:27.819
and social inequities.

00:17:27.819 --> 00:17:30.480
And so let's apply
this insight just

00:17:30.480 --> 00:17:32.800
for a moment to a
particular setting.

00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:35.310
Let's look at it in the
context of health care.

00:17:35.310 --> 00:17:38.280
Again, there's
mounds of research

00:17:38.280 --> 00:17:40.410
that really shows
the various ways

00:17:40.410 --> 00:17:43.180
in which racial discrimination
operates in our health care

00:17:43.180 --> 00:17:43.680
system.

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:46.110
And it's not simply at
the structural level,

00:17:46.110 --> 00:17:47.280
although that's important.

00:17:47.280 --> 00:17:49.950
That is at the level
of policies and laws.

00:17:49.950 --> 00:17:52.320
But also interpersonally,
in terms of the way

00:17:52.320 --> 00:17:56.370
that health care professionals
treat different patients.

00:17:56.370 --> 00:17:59.610
And one set of a part
of this literature

00:17:59.610 --> 00:18:02.760
is looking at how doctors
routinely underestimate

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:05.730
the pain of Black patients.

00:18:05.730 --> 00:18:08.760
And don't prescribe them
the necessary treatments

00:18:08.760 --> 00:18:10.540
and medication that
they might need.

00:18:10.540 --> 00:18:14.010
So a question for
us would be, what

00:18:14.010 --> 00:18:16.530
would it look like
to actually outsource

00:18:16.530 --> 00:18:18.480
these decisions to AI?

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:20.820
Would that make it more
fair, more equitable?

00:18:20.820 --> 00:18:25.080
Would that be a way to bypass
the existing social biases

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:28.780
and forms of discrimination
in our health care system?

00:18:28.780 --> 00:18:30.990
And so my recent work
has been wrestling

00:18:30.990 --> 00:18:34.470
with this and similar
questions in which I

00:18:34.470 --> 00:18:37.470
describe the combination
of coded bias

00:18:37.470 --> 00:18:41.460
and imagined objectivity
as the new Jim Code.

00:18:41.460 --> 00:18:45.120
Innovation that enables
social containment,

00:18:45.120 --> 00:18:48.660
while appearing fairer than
discriminatory practices

00:18:48.660 --> 00:18:50.270
of a previous era.

00:18:50.270 --> 00:18:53.500
What I'm drawing attention
to what this concept is

00:18:53.500 --> 00:18:57.610
that technology can actually
hide the ongoing nature

00:18:57.610 --> 00:18:59.320
of social domination
and allow it

00:18:59.320 --> 00:19:02.410
to penetrate every
facet of our lives

00:19:02.410 --> 00:19:04.780
under the guise of progress.

00:19:04.780 --> 00:19:07.030
And what I'm encouraging
us to think about

00:19:07.030 --> 00:19:11.680
is how anti-Blackness gets
encoded in and exercised

00:19:11.680 --> 00:19:14.260
through automated
systems by offering

00:19:14.260 --> 00:19:18.100
four conceptual offspring to the
new Jim Code that follow along

00:19:18.100 --> 00:19:19.570
kind of spectrum.

00:19:19.570 --> 00:19:23.740
From the most obvious
variety, engineered inequity,

00:19:23.740 --> 00:19:27.160
to the most insidious--
techno benevolence.

00:19:27.160 --> 00:19:28.630
And for the sake
of time, I'm just

00:19:28.630 --> 00:19:33.310
going to sketch the last element
of this, techno benevolence,

00:19:33.310 --> 00:19:35.830
and then move towards
some concluding thoughts.

00:19:35.830 --> 00:19:39.280
So techno benevolence
refers to those technologies

00:19:39.280 --> 00:19:43.060
that aim to address
bias in various ways.

00:19:43.060 --> 00:19:45.310
Take for example
new AI techniques

00:19:45.310 --> 00:19:47.330
for vetting job applicants.

00:19:47.330 --> 00:19:51.130
A company called Hirevue aims
to quote, "Reduce unconscious

00:19:51.130 --> 00:19:54.190
bias and promote
diversity in the workplace

00:19:54.190 --> 00:19:56.920
by using an AI
powered program that

00:19:56.920 --> 00:20:01.300
analyzes recorded interviews
of prospective employees."

00:20:01.300 --> 00:20:04.100
So it uses thousands
of data points,

00:20:04.100 --> 00:20:08.660
including verbal and nonverbal
cues like facial expression,

00:20:08.660 --> 00:20:10.870
posture, vocal tone.

00:20:10.870 --> 00:20:13.300
And then it compares
job seekers' scores

00:20:13.300 --> 00:20:17.290
to those of existing
top performing employees

00:20:17.290 --> 00:20:20.380
to decide who to flag
as a desirable hire

00:20:20.380 --> 00:20:23.050
and who to reject.

00:20:23.050 --> 00:20:25.900
Another value added,
according to Hirevue

00:20:25.900 --> 00:20:29.590
is that there's a lot that
a human interviewer misses

00:20:29.590 --> 00:20:32.410
that AI can keep track
of to make quote,

00:20:32.410 --> 00:20:35.080
"Data driven talent decisions."

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:38.320
After all, the problem of
employment discrimination

00:20:38.320 --> 00:20:40.750
is widespread and
well documented.

00:20:40.750 --> 00:20:44.350
So the logic goes, wouldn't
this be even more reason

00:20:44.350 --> 00:20:47.110
to outsource decisions to AI?

00:20:47.110 --> 00:20:49.930
Well consider that question
in light of a study

00:20:49.930 --> 00:20:52.420
by a Princeton team
of computer scientists

00:20:52.420 --> 00:20:56.200
that examined whether a
popular algorithm trained

00:20:56.200 --> 00:20:59.470
on human writing online
exhibited the same racially

00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:02.350
biased tendencies
that psychologists

00:21:02.350 --> 00:21:04.750
have documented among humans.

00:21:04.750 --> 00:21:08.170
In particular, they found
that the algorithm associated

00:21:08.170 --> 00:21:10.990
white sounding names
with pleasant words,

00:21:10.990 --> 00:21:13.870
and Black sounding names
with unpleasant ones.

00:21:13.870 --> 00:21:15.910
Which builds on a
classic audit study

00:21:15.910 --> 00:21:19.900
from about 2003, in which
two University of Chicago

00:21:19.900 --> 00:21:22.240
economists sent out
thousands of resumes

00:21:22.240 --> 00:21:24.910
to employers in
Boston and Chicago

00:21:24.910 --> 00:21:27.160
and just changed the
names on the resumes.

00:21:27.160 --> 00:21:29.530
Some had names like
Emily and Greg.

00:21:29.530 --> 00:21:32.320
Some names like
Lakisha and Jamal.

00:21:32.320 --> 00:21:35.290
And what they found was that
the white sounding names

00:21:35.290 --> 00:21:37.810
received 50% more callbacks.

00:21:37.810 --> 00:21:40.060
And so simply
turning to technology

00:21:40.060 --> 00:21:45.160
to solve that form of employment
bias is not going to save us.

00:21:45.160 --> 00:21:47.860
So too with gender
coded words and names.

00:21:47.860 --> 00:21:50.350
As Amazon learned
a few years ago

00:21:50.350 --> 00:21:53.050
when its own hiring
algorithm was found

00:21:53.050 --> 00:21:55.390
discriminating against women.

00:21:55.390 --> 00:21:57.400
Nevertheless, it
should be clear why

00:21:57.400 --> 00:22:01.810
technical fixes that claim
to bypass human biases

00:22:01.810 --> 00:22:03.640
are so desirable.

00:22:03.640 --> 00:22:06.910
If only there was a
way to slay centuries

00:22:06.910 --> 00:22:11.020
of racist and sexist demons
with a social justice bot.

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:13.750
Beyond desirable,
more like magical,

00:22:13.750 --> 00:22:16.120
magical for employers
perhaps looking

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:18.850
to streamline the grueling
work of recruitment,

00:22:18.850 --> 00:22:21.490
but a curse from
any job seekers.

00:22:21.490 --> 00:22:24.400
As this headline puts
it, "Your next interview

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:26.770
could be with a racist bot."

00:22:26.770 --> 00:22:30.520
Bringing us back to that
problem space we started with.

00:22:30.520 --> 00:22:33.640
So it's worth noting that
some job seekers are already

00:22:33.640 --> 00:22:37.810
developing ways to subvert
the system by trading answers

00:22:37.810 --> 00:22:41.080
to employers' tests, and
creating fake applications

00:22:41.080 --> 00:22:43.930
as informal audits of their own.

00:22:43.930 --> 00:22:48.070
In terms of public policy, the
algorithmic accountability bill

00:22:48.070 --> 00:22:52.150
is one effort in the US
to create some protections

00:22:52.150 --> 00:22:55.690
around the ubiquity of
automated and AI based decisions

00:22:55.690 --> 00:22:57.440
in our everyday lives.

00:22:57.440 --> 00:23:00.460
It's a start, but in
no way sufficient.

00:23:00.460 --> 00:23:03.310
Another development that
keeps me somewhat hopeful

00:23:03.310 --> 00:23:06.850
is that tech workers
themselves have increasingly

00:23:06.850 --> 00:23:09.940
been speaking out against
the most egregious forms

00:23:09.940 --> 00:23:13.970
of corporate collusion with
state sanctioned racism.

00:23:13.970 --> 00:23:17.890
For example, Microsoft employees
opposed to the company's ICE

00:23:17.890 --> 00:23:21.310
contract stated that,
"As the people who

00:23:21.310 --> 00:23:24.550
build the technologies that
Microsoft profits from,

00:23:24.550 --> 00:23:26.980
we refuse to be complicit."

00:23:26.980 --> 00:23:30.160
And in just the last few months,
the Alphabet Workers Union

00:23:30.160 --> 00:23:31.540
has been gaining momentum.

00:23:31.540 --> 00:23:33.943
And so for anyone in the
tech industry listening here,

00:23:33.943 --> 00:23:35.860
I would encourage you
to look up these efforts

00:23:35.860 --> 00:23:37.450
and stay connected.

00:23:37.450 --> 00:23:39.700
And as this article
published by Science

00:23:39.700 --> 00:23:42.310
for the People
reminds us, "Contrary

00:23:42.310 --> 00:23:44.290
to popular
narratives, organizing

00:23:44.290 --> 00:23:47.720
among technical workers
has a vibrant history,

00:23:47.720 --> 00:23:50.670
including engineers and
technicians in the '60s

00:23:50.670 --> 00:23:54.840
and '70s who fought
professionalism, individualism,

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:59.670
and reformism to contribute
to radical labor organizing."

00:23:59.670 --> 00:24:01.470
The current tech
workers movement,

00:24:01.470 --> 00:24:05.340
which includes students
across our many institutions,

00:24:05.340 --> 00:24:08.370
can draw from these past
organizers' strategies

00:24:08.370 --> 00:24:12.090
and challenges and learning
to navigate the contradictions

00:24:12.090 --> 00:24:15.630
and complexities of
organizing in tech today.

00:24:15.630 --> 00:24:18.840
In terms of education, which
I think of as the ground

00:24:18.840 --> 00:24:22.320
zero for planting a more
historically and socially

00:24:22.320 --> 00:24:25.050
situated approach
to STEM, I'll just

00:24:25.050 --> 00:24:28.560
mention one concrete resource
that you can download called,

00:24:28.560 --> 00:24:33.660
The Advancing Racial Literacy
In Tech Handbook, which

00:24:33.660 --> 00:24:36.480
was developed by some wonderful
colleagues at the Data

00:24:36.480 --> 00:24:39.330
and Society Research
Institute in New York.

00:24:39.330 --> 00:24:42.480
The aim of this
intervention is threefold--

00:24:42.480 --> 00:24:44.940
to develop an
intellectual understanding

00:24:44.940 --> 00:24:48.780
of how structural racism
operates in algorithms,

00:24:48.780 --> 00:24:53.430
social media platforms, and
technologies not yet developed.

00:24:53.430 --> 00:24:55.740
An emotional
intelligence concerning

00:24:55.740 --> 00:24:58.680
how to resolve racially
stressful situations

00:24:58.680 --> 00:25:01.800
within organizations,
and a commitment

00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:06.570
to take action to reduce
harms to communities of color.

00:25:06.570 --> 00:25:09.360
In that spirit,
initiatives like data

00:25:09.360 --> 00:25:12.390
for Black Lives and the
Detroit Community Tech Project

00:25:12.390 --> 00:25:15.540
are just two of the many
different tech justice

00:25:15.540 --> 00:25:18.870
organizations that exist
all over the world.

00:25:18.870 --> 00:25:20.850
Data for Black Lives
brings together

00:25:20.850 --> 00:25:24.420
people working across a number
of agencies and organizations

00:25:24.420 --> 00:25:27.340
in a proactive approach
to tech justice,

00:25:27.340 --> 00:25:29.460
especially at the policy level.

00:25:29.460 --> 00:25:31.890
And the Detroit Community
Technology Project

00:25:31.890 --> 00:25:34.680
develops and uses
technology rooted

00:25:34.680 --> 00:25:39.120
in community needs, offering
support to grassroots networks,

00:25:39.120 --> 00:25:40.950
doing data justice research.

00:25:40.950 --> 00:25:44.370
Including hosting what
they call Discotechs, which

00:25:44.370 --> 00:25:47.010
stands for discovering
technology,

00:25:47.010 --> 00:25:49.590
which are these multimedia
mobile and neighborhood

00:25:49.590 --> 00:25:53.460
workshop fairs that can be
adapted in other locales.

00:25:53.460 --> 00:25:56.610
In the end, it boils
down to whose knowledge?

00:25:56.610 --> 00:25:59.100
Or we might say
whose intelligence

00:25:59.100 --> 00:26:02.880
we're enfolding into our
socio-technical systems?

00:26:02.880 --> 00:26:05.190
Who are we listening to?

00:26:05.190 --> 00:26:07.770
Returning to the issue
of medical racism

00:26:07.770 --> 00:26:12.240
it turns out if AI is
trained on doctors' reports,

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:16.830
it will draw discriminatory
and faulty conclusions.

00:26:16.830 --> 00:26:20.550
According to the researchers,
we didn't train the algorithm

00:26:20.550 --> 00:26:23.250
to predict what the doctor was
going to say about the X-ray,

00:26:23.250 --> 00:26:25.950
we trained it to predict
what the patient was

00:26:25.950 --> 00:26:29.910
going to say about their own
experience of pain in the knee.

00:26:29.910 --> 00:26:33.240
And these predictions,
from the patients' reports

00:26:33.240 --> 00:26:35.130
were actually much
more accurate.

00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:36.810
Who would have guessed?

00:26:36.810 --> 00:26:39.700
So here's my final proposition.

00:26:39.700 --> 00:26:44.340
If it is the case that
inequity and injustice is

00:26:44.340 --> 00:26:47.800
woven into the very
fabric of our society,

00:26:47.800 --> 00:26:51.270
then that means each
twist, coil, and code

00:26:51.270 --> 00:26:55.530
is a chance for us to weave
new patterns, practices,

00:26:55.530 --> 00:26:57.030
and politics.

00:26:57.030 --> 00:26:59.790
The vastness of the problems
that we're up against

00:26:59.790 --> 00:27:02.520
will be their undoing
once we accept

00:27:02.520 --> 00:27:04.830
that we are pattern makers.

00:27:04.830 --> 00:27:06.750
And so if as I
suggested at the start

00:27:06.750 --> 00:27:09.930
an ahistoric and asocial
approach to science

00:27:09.930 --> 00:27:13.590
and technology
captures and contains,

00:27:13.590 --> 00:27:16.140
that means a historically
and socially grounded

00:27:16.140 --> 00:27:19.380
approach can open up
possibilities and pathways.

00:27:19.380 --> 00:27:23.580
It can create new settings,
and code new values,

00:27:23.580 --> 00:27:27.090
and build on critical
intellectual traditions that

00:27:27.090 --> 00:27:30.570
have continuously developed
insights and strategies

00:27:30.570 --> 00:27:32.610
grounded in justice.

00:27:32.610 --> 00:27:35.460
And my hope is that
we all find ways

00:27:35.460 --> 00:27:38.090
to build on this tradition.

00:27:38.090 --> 00:27:39.778
Thank you for your attention.

00:27:43.450 --> 00:27:47.070
So thank you so much, Ruha, for
the fascinating and incisive

00:27:47.070 --> 00:27:47.940
talk.

00:27:47.940 --> 00:27:50.160
And one of the things
I was struck by

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:55.260
was the way that you describe
the kind of benevolence,

00:27:55.260 --> 00:27:57.570
the so-called
benevolence approach

00:27:57.570 --> 00:28:01.740
that people might bring
to these communities,

00:28:01.740 --> 00:28:05.470
as if a kind of a savior
model in particular ways.

00:28:05.470 --> 00:28:09.900
So I wonder if you can
look at the other side

00:28:09.900 --> 00:28:11.670
and say a bit more
about what you

00:28:11.670 --> 00:28:14.160
see as community
projects that have

00:28:14.160 --> 00:28:16.200
been successful
that are emerging

00:28:16.200 --> 00:28:17.820
from within communities.

00:28:17.820 --> 00:28:21.060
And this is one of my students
has called critical community

00:28:21.060 --> 00:28:21.985
technologies.

00:28:21.985 --> 00:28:23.610
And what are the
conditions under which

00:28:23.610 --> 00:28:25.920
you've seen such
groups flourish where

00:28:25.920 --> 00:28:29.740
they have been the ones that
everything these technologies?

00:28:29.740 --> 00:28:31.270
Thank you so much, Fox.

00:28:31.270 --> 00:28:35.610
And thanks to all the organizers
behind the screen helping

00:28:35.610 --> 00:28:39.310
us to convene in this space
and this conversation.

00:28:39.310 --> 00:28:43.260
And so there's different ways
that this is taking shape.

00:28:43.260 --> 00:28:47.280
And I think that there's
no one set of conditions

00:28:47.280 --> 00:28:51.990
or requirements that I think
can make technology more

00:28:51.990 --> 00:28:54.560
empowering, less oppressive.

00:28:54.560 --> 00:28:57.600
The ones that I
find fascinating is

00:28:57.600 --> 00:29:01.290
when these digital
tools are turned back

00:29:01.290 --> 00:29:06.390
on those who wield power and
who monopolize resources.

00:29:06.390 --> 00:29:09.360
Sometimes in longer
versions of the talk,

00:29:09.360 --> 00:29:13.770
I'll mention a parody example
called the White Collar Early

00:29:13.770 --> 00:29:16.290
Warning System, which
is not community based,

00:29:16.290 --> 00:29:18.840
but it's an artistic, a
creative sort of rendering

00:29:18.840 --> 00:29:21.840
of what it means to take
these tools that are typically

00:29:21.840 --> 00:29:26.220
targeting the most
disenfranchised communities

00:29:26.220 --> 00:29:28.410
and turn it back to
those who produce

00:29:28.410 --> 00:29:31.600
risk for those very
same populations.

00:29:31.600 --> 00:29:35.010
And so there's examples
like that subversive turning

00:29:35.010 --> 00:29:39.030
of the digital tools that
sometimes enroll or collaborate

00:29:39.030 --> 00:29:40.890
with communities to make happen.

00:29:40.890 --> 00:29:43.620
Then there are the examples that
I hinted at like the Detroit

00:29:43.620 --> 00:29:45.990
Community Technology Project.

00:29:45.990 --> 00:29:51.330
There's a New York based version
of that in which it's really

00:29:51.330 --> 00:29:56.040
about generating meaningful
questions as a starting point

00:29:56.040 --> 00:29:57.990
for any kind of design project.

00:29:57.990 --> 00:30:00.960
I think sometimes what we think
about community collaboration,

00:30:00.960 --> 00:30:03.450
researchers, we huddle,
we come up with something

00:30:03.450 --> 00:30:05.400
and then we go out and
try to find communities

00:30:05.400 --> 00:30:06.720
to collaborate with.

00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:10.380
Rather than really starting at
ground zero with the generation

00:30:10.380 --> 00:30:11.070
of questions.

00:30:11.070 --> 00:30:15.450
To me, that's where so much
of the power is concentrated.

00:30:15.450 --> 00:30:18.840
The fact that people can't
ask meaningful questions

00:30:18.840 --> 00:30:20.760
about their lives,
about their world,

00:30:20.760 --> 00:30:23.890
and then have the resources
to produce the data,

00:30:23.890 --> 00:30:26.670
produce the tools that
would help them answer

00:30:26.670 --> 00:30:28.830
and intervene in those issues.

00:30:28.830 --> 00:30:32.010
And so one of the places that
I'm trying to work on that

00:30:32.010 --> 00:30:34.680
is at the Ida B.
Wells Just Data Lab

00:30:34.680 --> 00:30:37.800
here at Princeton
in which I have

00:30:37.800 --> 00:30:42.240
teams of undergrad students
who partner with communities

00:30:42.240 --> 00:30:45.150
to generate questions and then
the projects that come out

00:30:45.150 --> 00:30:49.840
of that as to me a
better starting point.

00:30:49.840 --> 00:30:52.590
So rather than us think about,
OK this is what we want to do,

00:30:52.590 --> 00:30:54.760
and then go find
community partners.

00:30:54.760 --> 00:30:56.970
So the Ida B.
Wells Just Data Lab

00:30:56.970 --> 00:30:59.250
is one example
housed in academia.

00:30:59.250 --> 00:31:01.830
But there are
other examples that

00:31:01.830 --> 00:31:06.360
come out of more policy
or advocacy, orientation.

00:31:06.360 --> 00:31:09.000
There are
anti-surveillance types

00:31:09.000 --> 00:31:13.470
of projects, some that are more
creative in which people are

00:31:13.470 --> 00:31:17.100
creating fashion that are
anti-surveillance fashion

00:31:17.100 --> 00:31:24.180
examples versus the LAPD's
coalition to stop surveillance

00:31:24.180 --> 00:31:26.680
in Los Angeles and other places.

00:31:26.680 --> 00:31:29.160
So I think the
take-home is that this

00:31:29.160 --> 00:31:31.600
can take many, many
different forms,

00:31:31.600 --> 00:31:36.540
from on a continuum of
more critical countering

00:31:36.540 --> 00:31:41.130
subversive to creative
self-fashioning and community

00:31:41.130 --> 00:31:44.160
based participatory research.

00:31:44.160 --> 00:31:47.310
Whether that's in the context
of environmental justice or data

00:31:47.310 --> 00:31:50.310
justice, there are
so many examples.

00:31:50.310 --> 00:31:52.620
And so for those who
are looking to plug-in,

00:31:52.620 --> 00:31:56.460
I would encourage them to
visit the Resources tab

00:31:56.460 --> 00:32:01.020
of my personal website,
ruhabenjamin.com/resources.

00:32:01.020 --> 00:32:04.440
And you'll see it, a
really long list of places

00:32:04.440 --> 00:32:07.170
to plug-in in your own backyard.

00:32:07.170 --> 00:32:10.200
And especially for the students
who might be listening.

00:32:10.200 --> 00:32:13.860
Sometimes when we are
awakened to issues like this,

00:32:13.860 --> 00:32:16.170
we want to start our own thing.

00:32:16.170 --> 00:32:18.180
The entrepreneurial
spirit and I would

00:32:18.180 --> 00:32:20.940
encourage us to plug into
things that already exist

00:32:20.940 --> 00:32:23.910
as a starting point,
rather than jump the gun

00:32:23.910 --> 00:32:26.100
and try to come
up the solutions.

00:32:26.100 --> 00:32:30.180
Going back to your savior,
the idea of a savior complex.

00:32:30.180 --> 00:32:33.060
And so I hope that
gives folks enough

00:32:33.060 --> 00:32:36.910
to chew on as a
place to get started.

00:32:36.910 --> 00:32:38.440
It does, it's so fascinating.

00:32:38.440 --> 00:32:41.170
And you bring to mind projects
such as, The Million Dollar

00:32:41.170 --> 00:32:42.040
Blocks Project.

00:32:42.040 --> 00:32:44.080
Looking at the amount
of money, mapping

00:32:44.080 --> 00:32:48.460
the amount of money used in
communities for incarceration,

00:32:48.460 --> 00:32:51.070
it prompts people to think
about other types of issues

00:32:51.070 --> 00:32:53.080
and where their money
could have gone.

00:32:53.080 --> 00:32:56.740
So it just seems like
exactly the kind of literacy

00:32:56.740 --> 00:32:59.230
and reversing the lens
that you describe.

00:32:59.230 --> 00:33:02.870
And so thank you, thank
you once again very much.

00:33:02.870 --> 00:33:05.200
And so next up, we
have Jon Kleinberg,

00:33:05.200 --> 00:33:08.180
professor at Cornell University
who is going to speak.

00:33:08.180 --> 00:33:10.960
And I think it's a very nice
compliment to what we've just

00:33:10.960 --> 00:33:15.850
seen from Professor Ruha
Benjamin, because he's looking

00:33:15.850 --> 00:33:19.270
in some sense under the
hood, but at the same time,

00:33:19.270 --> 00:33:23.470
bringing that kind of critical
lens around issues of fairness

00:33:23.470 --> 00:33:24.260
as well.

00:33:24.260 --> 00:33:28.390
So now let's welcome
our prerecorded video

00:33:28.390 --> 00:33:33.790
by Jon Kleinberg, followed again
by a brief, but clarifying Q&amp;A.

00:33:33.790 --> 00:33:37.960
It's a pleasure to be able to
take part in this event today.

00:33:37.960 --> 00:33:41.620
And I'll be talking about
topics in fairness and bias

00:33:41.620 --> 00:33:43.790
in algorithmics decision making.

00:33:43.790 --> 00:33:47.060
And I'll be approaching this
as a computer scientist.

00:33:47.060 --> 00:33:49.450
But what I'll be
talking about is

00:33:49.450 --> 00:33:52.480
joint work with my PhD
student, Manish Raghavan.

00:33:52.480 --> 00:33:57.670
And then with three researchers
from three other domains

00:33:57.670 --> 00:34:01.930
from Jens Ludwig in Public
Policy, Senhil Mullainathan

00:34:01.930 --> 00:34:04.720
in Behavioral Economist,
and Cass Sunstein,

00:34:04.720 --> 00:34:06.640
who's a professor of law.

00:34:06.640 --> 00:34:09.969
And in some sense to frame
what I'll be talking about,

00:34:09.969 --> 00:34:13.540
it's useful to actually go
back to a period of time

00:34:13.540 --> 00:34:18.969
when I was a PhD student
at MIT in the mid 1990s.

00:34:18.969 --> 00:34:20.530
And there was a
lot of excitement

00:34:20.530 --> 00:34:22.960
at that moment about the
way in which an idea that

00:34:22.960 --> 00:34:26.920
had been really ubiquitous
in fields like human computer

00:34:26.920 --> 00:34:29.620
interaction suddenly became
mainstream through much

00:34:29.620 --> 00:34:30.497
of computer science.

00:34:30.497 --> 00:34:32.080
And that was the
idea that the systems

00:34:32.080 --> 00:34:36.010
we were designing really
had these two different axes

00:34:36.010 --> 00:34:37.239
that we have to think about.

00:34:37.239 --> 00:34:39.070
There was the technical
axis, along which

00:34:39.070 --> 00:34:40.960
we had always organized things.

00:34:40.960 --> 00:34:43.903
But then there was also a
social dimension, right?

00:34:43.903 --> 00:34:45.320
Where we built
systems and we need

00:34:45.320 --> 00:34:50.980
to be sensitive to the social
and economic forces that

00:34:50.980 --> 00:34:51.699
underpin them.

00:34:51.699 --> 00:34:54.070
In some genuine sense, that
period through the 1990s,

00:34:54.070 --> 00:34:55.570
when we saw the
emergence of systems

00:34:55.570 --> 00:34:58.720
like the world
wide web, we really

00:34:58.720 --> 00:35:01.780
saw the building of
socio-technical systems.

00:35:01.780 --> 00:35:04.990
But over the past 10
years, particularly, we've

00:35:04.990 --> 00:35:09.670
been discovering that
these systems, even when

00:35:09.670 --> 00:35:11.470
we take both of
these into account,

00:35:11.470 --> 00:35:14.260
don't always produce the
outcomes that we were hoping.

00:35:14.260 --> 00:35:17.890
They end up being awash
in misinformation.

00:35:17.890 --> 00:35:20.530
They can lead to conflict
and polarization.

00:35:20.530 --> 00:35:22.720
They can produce
decisions that seem

00:35:22.720 --> 00:35:24.880
biased against particular
groups of people.

00:35:24.880 --> 00:35:27.730
It's almost as though there's
this third dimension that we

00:35:27.730 --> 00:35:29.290
haven't been taking
into account,

00:35:29.290 --> 00:35:31.930
and much of the momentum
in the past 10 years

00:35:31.930 --> 00:35:34.570
of research at the
interface of computing

00:35:34.570 --> 00:35:36.610
and the social
sciences has really

00:35:36.610 --> 00:35:39.490
been trying to explore what
this third dimension might

00:35:39.490 --> 00:35:40.000
look like.

00:35:40.000 --> 00:35:42.640
And if I were to
summarize, I might

00:35:42.640 --> 00:35:44.140
say it's almost
as though there's

00:35:44.140 --> 00:35:46.360
this normative dimension
that we've been missing

00:35:46.360 --> 00:35:47.980
in the design of our systems.

00:35:47.980 --> 00:35:50.740
That even if we fully
understand, which we certainly

00:35:50.740 --> 00:35:54.970
don't, the social and
technical forces at play, even

00:35:54.970 --> 00:35:57.430
with all of that
it does not tell us

00:35:57.430 --> 00:35:59.590
what outcomes we should
be aiming for, right?

00:35:59.590 --> 00:36:02.950
What we might want as a society.

00:36:02.950 --> 00:36:05.800
That, in the end, is
a different question.

00:36:05.800 --> 00:36:08.410
And so I want to talk about
from the computer science

00:36:08.410 --> 00:36:09.970
perspective how
some of these issues

00:36:09.970 --> 00:36:12.910
have come into play in
the algorithms, the AI

00:36:12.910 --> 00:36:17.560
systems, and the machine
learning protocols that we

00:36:17.560 --> 00:36:19.900
have been trying to deploy.

00:36:19.900 --> 00:36:22.787
Much of this begins
with a observation

00:36:22.787 --> 00:36:24.620
that people have been
making that's gathered

00:36:24.620 --> 00:36:27.620
increasing amount of force in
the past few years as well.

00:36:27.620 --> 00:36:30.130
Which is to take
ideas that we have

00:36:30.130 --> 00:36:33.850
been very good at executing
in the online world, things

00:36:33.850 --> 00:36:36.160
like ranking, things
like recommendation

00:36:36.160 --> 00:36:39.790
as in this help screen
from a Netflix page.

00:36:39.790 --> 00:36:43.600
Where we take
people online and we

00:36:43.600 --> 00:36:46.210
with complex sets of
tastes and interests,

00:36:46.210 --> 00:36:49.118
we produce feature
vectors to represent them.

00:36:49.118 --> 00:36:50.660
And then we try to
make a prediction.

00:36:50.660 --> 00:36:53.050
For example, a prediction
about whether they

00:36:53.050 --> 00:36:55.570
will like this particular movie,
or what is the probability

00:36:55.570 --> 00:36:57.340
they will like this movie?

00:36:57.340 --> 00:36:59.620
And people began
asking, if we are

00:36:59.620 --> 00:37:02.230
so good at doing this
in the online world,

00:37:02.230 --> 00:37:05.260
let's think about situations
in the offline world

00:37:05.260 --> 00:37:07.570
where there's something
that looks syntactically

00:37:07.570 --> 00:37:09.075
similar going on.

00:37:09.075 --> 00:37:11.200
So let's think about
something that's superficially

00:37:11.200 --> 00:37:14.267
is quite different, which is
the act of applying for a job.

00:37:14.267 --> 00:37:15.850
If we think about
it, we take somebody

00:37:15.850 --> 00:37:18.130
with their complex
past work experience.

00:37:18.130 --> 00:37:21.430
They produce a tabular
feature-like representation

00:37:21.430 --> 00:37:23.590
in the form of a resume.

00:37:23.590 --> 00:37:28.300
They submit it to a human hiring
committee that evaluates it.

00:37:28.300 --> 00:37:29.710
A very similar pipeline.

00:37:29.710 --> 00:37:33.340
And that committee then
makes some kind of a--

00:37:33.340 --> 00:37:34.757
well, it's not
clear what they do.

00:37:34.757 --> 00:37:36.340
They evaluate some
objective function?

00:37:36.340 --> 00:37:37.430
Do they make a prediction?

00:37:37.430 --> 00:37:39.100
This is actually where
it gets complicated,

00:37:39.100 --> 00:37:40.720
and that tension is
actually what I'll

00:37:40.720 --> 00:37:42.880
be trying to get at in my talk.

00:37:42.880 --> 00:37:45.730
A similar thing happens with
the college admissions process.

00:37:45.730 --> 00:37:49.300
You take someone's grade 9
through 12 educational history,

00:37:49.300 --> 00:37:51.070
their interests,
and so forth, turn

00:37:51.070 --> 00:37:52.540
into a feature representation.

00:37:52.540 --> 00:37:55.180
And again, a committee might
make a decision with an unclear

00:37:55.180 --> 00:37:56.510
objective function.

00:37:56.510 --> 00:38:00.340
So many things following
this kind of template, right?

00:38:00.340 --> 00:38:03.440
Things that we might
call screening decisions.

00:38:03.440 --> 00:38:05.980
So when somebody
applies for a job,

00:38:05.980 --> 00:38:09.430
there has to be a decision
made, educational admission

00:38:09.430 --> 00:38:11.440
decisions, and
many other domains.

00:38:11.440 --> 00:38:17.230
The applications for a loan
in the domain of credit,

00:38:17.230 --> 00:38:20.980
even in the criminal justice
system where the law instructs

00:38:20.980 --> 00:38:23.770
a judge at a pretrial
detention hearing

00:38:23.770 --> 00:38:28.390
to make a prediction
about the likelihood

00:38:28.390 --> 00:38:30.910
that a defendant will return
for their court appearance

00:38:30.910 --> 00:38:33.710
without committing a
crime in the meantime.

00:38:33.710 --> 00:38:36.370
And so these are all what
you might call high stakes

00:38:36.370 --> 00:38:37.070
decisions.

00:38:37.070 --> 00:38:40.600
These are decisions where the
outcome matters significantly

00:38:40.600 --> 00:38:43.400
to the individual
being screened, right?

00:38:43.400 --> 00:38:45.190
So it matters a lot
if you get this job

00:38:45.190 --> 00:38:46.982
you apply for, if you
get into this college

00:38:46.982 --> 00:38:49.730
you apply for, and in
all these other examples.

00:38:49.730 --> 00:38:51.970
This is not to say that
ranking and recommendation

00:38:51.970 --> 00:38:58.395
of online content are somehow
overall low stakes activities.

00:38:58.395 --> 00:38:59.770
In the end, it
certainly does not

00:38:59.770 --> 00:39:01.180
matter whether
Netflix gets right

00:39:01.180 --> 00:39:02.470
whether I like this movie.

00:39:02.470 --> 00:39:04.970
In the end, it doesn't matter
whether this particular search

00:39:04.970 --> 00:39:08.080
results page has the result I
was particularly looking for.

00:39:08.080 --> 00:39:10.330
But we've also seen
over the past few years

00:39:10.330 --> 00:39:13.960
that you can take 100 billion
of these low stakes decisions--

00:39:13.960 --> 00:39:16.690
search results, news
feed decisions, and they

00:39:16.690 --> 00:39:19.060
can add up to something that
is extremely high stakes,

00:39:19.060 --> 00:39:21.220
like the formation
of public opinion

00:39:21.220 --> 00:39:25.030
as part of the political
process in a large country.

00:39:25.030 --> 00:39:27.250
So everything that we do
in this domain I think

00:39:27.250 --> 00:39:28.840
has giant ramifications.

00:39:28.840 --> 00:39:29.920
The stakes are high.

00:39:29.920 --> 00:39:31.960
I focus on these
screen decisions

00:39:31.960 --> 00:39:33.760
as being individually
high stakes.

00:39:33.760 --> 00:39:36.820
Each individual decision
is actually significant.

00:39:36.820 --> 00:39:38.830
And because of this,
it's very natural

00:39:38.830 --> 00:39:41.950
and research has turned to this
question of the risks of bias

00:39:41.950 --> 00:39:43.840
in these kinds of decisions.

00:39:43.840 --> 00:39:46.180
When we deploy
algorithms to make them,

00:39:46.180 --> 00:39:48.710
where would we deploy groups
of humans to make them?

00:39:48.710 --> 00:39:50.960
And so if we're going to
think about the ways in which

00:39:50.960 --> 00:39:53.883
algorithms might introduce
bias into this process,

00:39:53.883 --> 00:39:55.300
we should really
start by thinking

00:39:55.300 --> 00:39:58.090
about what we know about human
bias in these situations,

00:39:58.090 --> 00:40:03.220
because human bias is a profound
factor in all of these domains.

00:40:03.220 --> 00:40:05.530
Now well there's
been extensive amount

00:40:05.530 --> 00:40:08.890
of research in the behavioral
sciences on human biases,

00:40:08.890 --> 00:40:14.140
and including an explosion of
work in the 1990s on topics

00:40:14.140 --> 00:40:17.680
in implicit bias.

00:40:17.680 --> 00:40:21.190
And a number of now
classical investigations

00:40:21.190 --> 00:40:23.560
looked at this
question empirically.

00:40:23.560 --> 00:40:28.400
And they found things that are
illustrated by, for example,

00:40:28.400 --> 00:40:31.100
this graph on the right
here, which I'll explain.

00:40:31.100 --> 00:40:34.040
This comes from a
paper in Nature in 1997

00:40:34.040 --> 00:40:37.640
by the European social
scientist, Christine Wennenas

00:40:37.640 --> 00:40:39.020
and Agnes Wold.

00:40:39.020 --> 00:40:42.080
And so they were interested
in the disparate impact

00:40:42.080 --> 00:40:45.445
of screening decisions,
as we are here today.

00:40:45.445 --> 00:40:46.820
And so what they
did was they got

00:40:46.820 --> 00:40:51.530
records of decisions that
were made on European research

00:40:51.530 --> 00:40:53.240
commission grant proposals.

00:40:53.240 --> 00:40:56.510
And as part of that process,
the reviewer of the proposal

00:40:56.510 --> 00:40:59.090
was supposed to assign a
so-called competence score

00:40:59.090 --> 00:41:00.660
to the principal investigator.

00:41:00.660 --> 00:41:03.380
How competent are they
to do the research?

00:41:03.380 --> 00:41:05.840
And so that's some kind
of a numerical score.

00:41:05.840 --> 00:41:08.240
And Vanegas and Vold were
interested in this case

00:41:08.240 --> 00:41:10.490
in potential disperate
impacts and the assignment

00:41:10.490 --> 00:41:14.480
of this competent
score by gender.

00:41:14.480 --> 00:41:18.170
And so what they
did was they looked

00:41:18.170 --> 00:41:20.367
at many different measures
of external impact

00:41:20.367 --> 00:41:21.200
for this researcher.

00:41:21.200 --> 00:41:24.320
They looked at publications,
a number of publications

00:41:24.320 --> 00:41:26.040
in high profile
venues, citations

00:41:26.040 --> 00:41:29.420
to those publications, success
at past European research

00:41:29.420 --> 00:41:31.550
commission grant
proposals, and so forth.

00:41:31.550 --> 00:41:34.070
And they put that on the x-axis.

00:41:34.070 --> 00:41:37.910
And then on the y-axis, they
plotted the competence score.

00:41:37.910 --> 00:41:40.820
And what we see here is that
if I pick a particular point

00:41:40.820 --> 00:41:44.390
on the x-axis, a particular
value of the external impact

00:41:44.390 --> 00:41:46.280
and I start to
move vertically, we

00:41:46.280 --> 00:41:49.757
see that the typical
competence score for principle

00:41:49.757 --> 00:41:51.590
investigators who were
men was significantly

00:41:51.590 --> 00:41:54.770
higher than for women.

00:41:54.770 --> 00:41:57.860
Or if I were to pick a
particular point on the y-axis

00:41:57.860 --> 00:42:00.680
and I move horizontally,
then what I find

00:42:00.680 --> 00:42:03.915
is that for a particular
competence score,

00:42:03.915 --> 00:42:05.540
women as principal
investigators needed

00:42:05.540 --> 00:42:07.550
significantly higher
measures of external impact

00:42:07.550 --> 00:42:08.900
in order to achieve that.

00:42:08.900 --> 00:42:13.940
And this was one of many studies
around this time that tried

00:42:13.940 --> 00:42:15.080
this research methodology.

00:42:15.080 --> 00:42:18.770
Find people who are
similar in the features

00:42:18.770 --> 00:42:20.862
that they are presenting
to the decision

00:42:20.862 --> 00:42:23.070
and are nonetheless getting
a very different outcome.

00:42:23.070 --> 00:42:26.030
And thereby discovering some of
the ways in which this breaks

00:42:26.030 --> 00:42:30.560
down along the lines of what the
law considers to be protected

00:42:30.560 --> 00:42:32.070
attributes.

00:42:32.070 --> 00:42:35.150
So this is an
illustration of human bias

00:42:35.150 --> 00:42:37.370
in an empirical context.

00:42:37.370 --> 00:42:40.370
Now when we move to
algorithms, and we consider

00:42:40.370 --> 00:42:43.310
the idea of algorithms making
similar decisions that we might

00:42:43.310 --> 00:42:46.640
train an algorithm to engage
in a predictive activity

00:42:46.640 --> 00:42:50.300
to make a decision like this,
we face a different sort

00:42:50.300 --> 00:42:51.530
of situation.

00:42:51.530 --> 00:42:53.690
First, algorithms
in contrast humans,

00:42:53.690 --> 00:42:57.180
have no direct incentives
to exhibit bias.

00:42:57.180 --> 00:42:59.060
That's essentially
by definition.

00:42:59.060 --> 00:43:01.172
The algorithm after
all knows really

00:43:01.172 --> 00:43:02.630
in the end nothing
about the world.

00:43:02.630 --> 00:43:04.160
It doesn't know who you are.

00:43:04.160 --> 00:43:05.660
It's simply been
trained to optimize

00:43:05.660 --> 00:43:06.890
this objective function.

00:43:06.890 --> 00:43:09.555
Despite this, what we've
learned in the past

00:43:09.555 --> 00:43:11.180
several years of
research is that there

00:43:11.180 --> 00:43:14.730
are many sources of
potential bias in algorithms.

00:43:14.730 --> 00:43:17.660
And we can try conceptually
to decompose these

00:43:17.660 --> 00:43:19.890
into some constituent parts.

00:43:19.890 --> 00:43:22.018
So for example, the choice
of label or objective

00:43:22.018 --> 00:43:23.810
function that I'm trying
to predict, right?

00:43:23.810 --> 00:43:25.520
The target of my prediction.

00:43:25.520 --> 00:43:27.770
And we can see how
this might happen.

00:43:27.770 --> 00:43:31.070
Suppose, for
example, that I were

00:43:31.070 --> 00:43:34.190
trying to train an algorithm
to assign a competence

00:43:34.190 --> 00:43:36.980
score in evaluating European
research commission grant

00:43:36.980 --> 00:43:37.790
proposals.

00:43:37.790 --> 00:43:41.480
I might feed it training data,
just the administrative data

00:43:41.480 --> 00:43:44.240
of the administration of
this program over many years

00:43:44.240 --> 00:43:46.970
what scores were assigned
to which grant proposals.

00:43:46.970 --> 00:43:49.820
In the process, it would learn
to make these predictions,

00:43:49.820 --> 00:43:52.520
and it might pick
up on, therefore,

00:43:52.520 --> 00:43:56.240
the gender disparity that
was already in the data.

00:43:56.240 --> 00:43:59.210
It's simply trying to faithfully
approximate the training data

00:43:59.210 --> 00:44:00.260
that it's been given.

00:44:00.260 --> 00:44:04.800
Similarly, we could see that
in the features which I handed,

00:44:04.800 --> 00:44:05.300
right?

00:44:05.300 --> 00:44:07.978
If I'm trying to have
it predict other things,

00:44:07.978 --> 00:44:10.520
but one thing that I handed is
the so-called competence score

00:44:10.520 --> 00:44:12.350
of the researcher,
then it essentially

00:44:12.350 --> 00:44:15.830
has a feature that already
has that bias built into it.

00:44:15.830 --> 00:44:20.040
These are the kinds of
points that we think about.

00:44:20.040 --> 00:44:24.350
Now if we want to look at
sources of bias in algorithms,

00:44:24.350 --> 00:44:27.590
then we face an
interpretability challenge.

00:44:27.590 --> 00:44:30.620
We have to actually be able
to look into the algorithm

00:44:30.620 --> 00:44:34.290
and try to understand
what's going on.

00:44:34.290 --> 00:44:36.140
But a first point
I'd like to make

00:44:36.140 --> 00:44:41.270
is that interpretability is not
only an issue for algorithms.

00:44:41.270 --> 00:44:43.880
In fact, much of
discrimination law

00:44:43.880 --> 00:44:47.030
is actually built, if you
think about it, with the idea

00:44:47.030 --> 00:44:48.950
that human decision-making
is likewise

00:44:48.950 --> 00:44:51.530
inscrutable and uninterpretable.

00:44:51.530 --> 00:44:53.090
Because in the end,
the law actually

00:44:53.090 --> 00:44:56.240
recognizes two categories
of discrimination.

00:44:56.240 --> 00:45:00.740
This is in US
discrimination law where

00:45:00.740 --> 00:45:02.450
the terminology is
disparate treatment

00:45:02.450 --> 00:45:03.860
versus disparate impact.

00:45:03.860 --> 00:45:06.230
Disparate treatment,
deliberately favoring

00:45:06.230 --> 00:45:09.350
one applicant say that our
running example of something

00:45:09.350 --> 00:45:11.900
like hiring, based
on a protected

00:45:11.900 --> 00:45:15.030
attributes like race, gender,
national origin, religion,

00:45:15.030 --> 00:45:16.310
or age.

00:45:16.310 --> 00:45:18.980
And separately,
disparate impact,

00:45:18.980 --> 00:45:21.740
the notion that
regardless of intent,

00:45:21.740 --> 00:45:25.490
if a screening decision has a
disproportionate adverse effect

00:45:25.490 --> 00:45:28.430
on a protected group,
then the burden of proof

00:45:28.430 --> 00:45:30.950
shifts to the decision maker
to establish the business

00:45:30.950 --> 00:45:33.460
necessity of their decision.

00:45:33.460 --> 00:45:35.540
Such that if I
were hiring people

00:45:35.540 --> 00:45:38.270
for a job that regularly
involved reaching things

00:45:38.270 --> 00:45:40.880
in extremely high
places, then I might

00:45:40.880 --> 00:45:43.280
want employees who were
taller, even if that

00:45:43.280 --> 00:45:45.680
has a disproportionate adverse
effect based on gender,

00:45:45.680 --> 00:45:46.710
for example.

00:45:46.710 --> 00:45:48.405
But if I were hiring
software engineers,

00:45:48.405 --> 00:45:50.030
and I put in a height
requirement, then

00:45:50.030 --> 00:45:52.113
it'd be very, very hard
to justify that on grounds

00:45:52.113 --> 00:45:54.050
of business necessity.

00:45:54.050 --> 00:45:57.560
Now the point is, it can be
very hard to tell if a decision

00:45:57.560 --> 00:45:59.690
maker has engaged in
disparate treatment

00:45:59.690 --> 00:46:01.650
or in disparate impact.

00:46:01.650 --> 00:46:04.080
And there are a few
reasons for this.

00:46:04.080 --> 00:46:07.520
The most immediate is that
it's very hard to ask people,

00:46:07.520 --> 00:46:09.980
they might lie.

00:46:09.980 --> 00:46:13.370
They might be deceptive in the
evidence that they present.

00:46:13.370 --> 00:46:14.810
But there's also
a deeper reason,

00:46:14.810 --> 00:46:16.935
a reason coming from the
behavioral sciences, which

00:46:16.935 --> 00:46:19.640
is they might genuinely
not know the rationale they

00:46:19.640 --> 00:46:22.110
used for their decision.

00:46:22.110 --> 00:46:22.610
Right?

00:46:22.610 --> 00:46:25.480
And so there's
actually a long history

00:46:25.480 --> 00:46:28.873
of work in the behavioral
sciences establishing

00:46:28.873 --> 00:46:30.790
that when people provide
you with explanations

00:46:30.790 --> 00:46:33.490
for their decisions,
those may or may not

00:46:33.490 --> 00:46:36.130
be accurate renditions
of what actually

00:46:36.130 --> 00:46:37.450
went into their decision.

00:46:37.450 --> 00:46:38.980
How could we possibly know that?

00:46:38.980 --> 00:46:41.560
Well some of this work
goes back to a series

00:46:41.560 --> 00:46:44.320
of very creative experiments
done by Nisbett and Wilson

00:46:44.320 --> 00:46:48.140
in the 1970s where they
would create lab situations,

00:46:48.140 --> 00:46:49.930
ask people to make decisions.

00:46:49.930 --> 00:46:51.700
And then with the
second group, they

00:46:51.700 --> 00:46:54.040
would subtly change some
future of the environment,

00:46:54.040 --> 00:46:56.470
get the decision to
change, and then ask people

00:46:56.470 --> 00:46:58.240
why they made their decision.

00:46:58.240 --> 00:46:59.903
And often people's
explanations had

00:46:59.903 --> 00:47:02.320
nothing to do with the one
feature of the environment that

00:47:02.320 --> 00:47:03.480
changed.

00:47:03.480 --> 00:47:04.480
That's kind of abstract.

00:47:04.480 --> 00:47:06.940
So let me make that very
concrete with an example

00:47:06.940 --> 00:47:08.410
from one of their experiments.

00:47:08.410 --> 00:47:11.980
One of their experiments, they
gave people a word recall task.

00:47:11.980 --> 00:47:15.400
So they would ask people to
memorize a list of words,

00:47:15.400 --> 00:47:16.900
recite it back, and
then at the end,

00:47:16.900 --> 00:47:19.180
they would ask a non
sequitur question-- name

00:47:19.180 --> 00:47:21.010
a brand of laundry detergent.

00:47:21.010 --> 00:47:24.280
OK, in group B, they
would do the same thing.

00:47:24.280 --> 00:47:27.730
But in the word recall to ask,
the words they would ask people

00:47:27.730 --> 00:47:29.410
to memorize where things like--

00:47:29.410 --> 00:47:34.733
beach, ocean, moon,
waves, sea, and so forth.

00:47:34.733 --> 00:47:37.150
And then when they said name
a brand of laundry detergent,

00:47:37.150 --> 00:47:39.140
many more people said Tide.

00:47:39.140 --> 00:47:41.230
Now this is the
effect of priming.

00:47:41.230 --> 00:47:43.397
Priming is that if I tell
you a bunch of words,

00:47:43.397 --> 00:47:44.980
and then I ask you
to think of a word,

00:47:44.980 --> 00:47:47.522
you often think of a word that's
right in the vicinity of all

00:47:47.522 --> 00:47:51.040
those words by the process
of free association.

00:47:51.040 --> 00:47:52.970
But Nisbett and Wilson
did one more step,

00:47:52.970 --> 00:47:54.700
which was extremely clever.

00:47:54.700 --> 00:47:58.120
They asked people, why did
you pick that brand of laundry

00:47:58.120 --> 00:47:59.110
detergent?

00:47:59.110 --> 00:48:01.630
And in both groups A
and B, both conditions

00:48:01.630 --> 00:48:03.265
of the experiment,
people said it's

00:48:03.265 --> 00:48:05.682
because it's the one my family
used when I was growing up.

00:48:05.682 --> 00:48:06.950
It's what we use at home.

00:48:06.950 --> 00:48:09.100
I remember the logo
from the store.

00:48:09.100 --> 00:48:10.540
People in the
second group did not

00:48:10.540 --> 00:48:12.670
say it's because you primed
me with a lot of words

00:48:12.670 --> 00:48:14.140
that made me think of Tide.

00:48:14.140 --> 00:48:16.930
Even though, statistically, we
know that many of those people

00:48:16.930 --> 00:48:18.040
must have been
primed because there

00:48:18.040 --> 00:48:19.665
was a big difference
in how many people

00:48:19.665 --> 00:48:21.680
said Tide in the two groups.

00:48:21.680 --> 00:48:25.773
So there are many other
Nisbett, Wilson experiments.

00:48:25.773 --> 00:48:27.190
In a different
one, you ask people

00:48:27.190 --> 00:48:28.870
to choose clothes from a rack.

00:48:28.870 --> 00:48:31.990
You orient the rack to get them
to choose the one on the right

00:48:31.990 --> 00:48:33.142
with high probability.

00:48:33.142 --> 00:48:34.600
Yet, when people
are asked why they

00:48:34.600 --> 00:48:35.920
chose the article
of clothing, they

00:48:35.920 --> 00:48:37.900
mention the style or the
color, and not the fact

00:48:37.900 --> 00:48:39.942
that you had oriented
things to essentially force

00:48:39.942 --> 00:48:41.390
that one on them.

00:48:41.390 --> 00:48:44.200
In other words, what's
essentially going on here

00:48:44.200 --> 00:48:47.020
is that it's a set
of findings that

00:48:47.020 --> 00:48:48.850
should make us think
a little harder

00:48:48.850 --> 00:48:50.970
about the notion
of interpretability

00:48:50.970 --> 00:48:53.740
and inscrutability as we
often apply it to algorithms.

00:48:53.740 --> 00:48:55.750
Because it says although
people are always

00:48:55.750 --> 00:48:58.450
happy to give you the
sense of an explanation,

00:48:58.450 --> 00:49:00.923
they're always happy to say
this is why I did something.

00:49:00.923 --> 00:49:03.340
The harder question is to know
whether that explanation is

00:49:03.340 --> 00:49:04.570
correct.

00:49:04.570 --> 00:49:06.610
In some sense, humans
in their own way

00:49:06.610 --> 00:49:09.800
are inscrutable or black boxes
in their decision making.

00:49:09.800 --> 00:49:12.490
We can't always know why
they did what they did.

00:49:12.490 --> 00:49:15.190
And that we argue
actually presents

00:49:15.190 --> 00:49:17.020
an interesting opportunity.

00:49:17.020 --> 00:49:20.290
Beyond the question of whether
algorithms are increasing

00:49:20.290 --> 00:49:22.780
or decreasing the
level of discrimination

00:49:22.780 --> 00:49:25.660
in a particular situation,
which is an important question

00:49:25.660 --> 00:49:28.090
that the field has
been thinking about,

00:49:28.090 --> 00:49:30.130
we argue there's a
second question, which

00:49:30.130 --> 00:49:33.460
is the ways in which well
regulated algorithms can

00:49:33.460 --> 00:49:37.015
make discrimination
easier to detect.

00:49:37.015 --> 00:49:38.890
The point being that
even an algorithm that's

00:49:38.890 --> 00:49:41.560
extremely complex, trained
through an extremely

00:49:41.560 --> 00:49:44.260
long pipeline of training with
very large amounts of training

00:49:44.260 --> 00:49:47.710
data, despite all of that,
it has certain properties

00:49:47.710 --> 00:49:50.710
that we can never expect
from human decision makers.

00:49:50.710 --> 00:49:55.265
Obviously you know it's not
that we can simply read the code

00:49:55.265 --> 00:49:56.890
and expect to understand
the algorithm.

00:49:56.890 --> 00:49:59.510
There all sorts of reasons
ranging from the difficulty,

00:49:59.510 --> 00:50:02.980
the cognitive difficulty of
reading code, to formal results

00:50:02.980 --> 00:50:04.572
and undesirability
that tell us that.

00:50:04.572 --> 00:50:06.280
But we can do some
things you could never

00:50:06.280 --> 00:50:07.370
do with a human being.

00:50:07.370 --> 00:50:09.310
You can for example,
if the algorithm is

00:50:09.310 --> 00:50:12.610
available for inspection,
examine the objective function

00:50:12.610 --> 00:50:14.530
that's being optimized.

00:50:14.530 --> 00:50:16.150
Whereas with human
decision makers,

00:50:16.150 --> 00:50:17.830
we can never really
ask somebody,

00:50:17.830 --> 00:50:19.163
what is your objective function?

00:50:19.163 --> 00:50:21.455
Think of all the situations
which you've been called on

00:50:21.455 --> 00:50:23.628
to evaluate people, you
would have a hard time

00:50:23.628 --> 00:50:25.420
writing down your formal
objective function

00:50:25.420 --> 00:50:26.620
in that situation.

00:50:26.620 --> 00:50:28.900
We can provide the algorithm
with counterfactuals.

00:50:28.900 --> 00:50:31.310
We can say, here's
somebody who was evaluated.

00:50:31.310 --> 00:50:33.220
I'm going to change
certain features,

00:50:33.220 --> 00:50:35.220
and I'm going to run this
through the classifier

00:50:35.220 --> 00:50:37.480
again and see if
the result changes.

00:50:37.480 --> 00:50:39.460
Again, with a human
being, you could

00:50:39.460 --> 00:50:42.670
ask somebody would you
have made the same decision

00:50:42.670 --> 00:50:45.490
if this person had graduated
from school X instead of school

00:50:45.490 --> 00:50:48.065
Y. And they might be even
in good faith attempt

00:50:48.065 --> 00:50:49.690
to give you an answer
to that question.

00:50:49.690 --> 00:50:51.857
But there's no way to really
know if that's actually

00:50:51.857 --> 00:50:52.990
what they would have done.

00:50:52.990 --> 00:50:55.030
So the point is,
algorithms aren't just

00:50:55.030 --> 00:50:59.200
a change in accuracy, or scale,
or quantity relative to humans.

00:50:59.200 --> 00:51:01.360
They're doing something
dramatically different.

00:51:01.360 --> 00:51:04.420
They have an explicit
formal process

00:51:04.420 --> 00:51:06.910
that they're following
with explicit features,

00:51:06.910 --> 00:51:08.830
an explicit objective
function, something

00:51:08.830 --> 00:51:11.620
that we can actually trace, even
if we can't follow the code.

00:51:11.620 --> 00:51:16.030
And so we can prove them almost
as a natural experiment in ways

00:51:16.030 --> 00:51:18.530
that we could never
do with human beings.

00:51:18.530 --> 00:51:21.590
And so in this final couple
of minutes of the talk,

00:51:21.590 --> 00:51:23.230
I wanted to ask
what kinds of things

00:51:23.230 --> 00:51:27.260
might we learn if we can probe
an algorithm in this way?

00:51:27.260 --> 00:51:29.470
And so I'll introduce
a bit of notation

00:51:29.470 --> 00:51:30.790
to be able to talk about this.

00:51:30.790 --> 00:51:33.460
But it leads to the observation
that this ways in which we

00:51:33.460 --> 00:51:36.940
can decompose algorithmic bias
can actually be made formally

00:51:36.940 --> 00:51:38.510
in a certain sense.

00:51:38.510 --> 00:51:40.840
So how might we
make this formal?

00:51:40.840 --> 00:51:43.872
Well let's think about a
machine learning pipeline.

00:51:43.872 --> 00:51:46.330
And again, let's think about
the notion of a well regulated

00:51:46.330 --> 00:51:49.270
algorithm, meaning that
the features, the training

00:51:49.270 --> 00:51:51.910
process, the objective function
are available for inspection.

00:51:51.910 --> 00:51:54.830
That's clearly crucial
in any of this analysis.

00:51:54.830 --> 00:51:58.270
So we have individuals,
they have feature vectors.

00:51:58.270 --> 00:52:01.540
We have something that the
screener is trying to optimize,

00:52:01.540 --> 00:52:04.210
let's call it a
productivity function.

00:52:04.210 --> 00:52:05.750
We're concerned about bias.

00:52:05.750 --> 00:52:08.275
So we have an advantage
group, called it A,

00:52:08.275 --> 00:52:10.870
and a disadvantage
group, let's call it D.

00:52:10.870 --> 00:52:14.530
And finally, the frequency
of different feature vectors

00:52:14.530 --> 00:52:17.140
in these populations
are different.

00:52:17.140 --> 00:52:19.160
So the frequency of
feature vector X in group A

00:52:19.160 --> 00:52:24.220
some function P of XA and
in group D it's P of XD.

00:52:24.220 --> 00:52:28.268
So the point might be that
given the full feature vector X,

00:52:28.268 --> 00:52:30.310
it doesn't matter what
group somebody comes from,

00:52:30.310 --> 00:52:32.920
the advantage group or
the disadvantage group.

00:52:32.920 --> 00:52:34.810
Because X contains
everything we need

00:52:34.810 --> 00:52:36.893
to evaluates the
productivity function.

00:52:36.893 --> 00:52:39.310
Nevertheless, there might be
a disparity between these two

00:52:39.310 --> 00:52:41.620
groups, because of the
way those feature vectors

00:52:41.620 --> 00:52:43.090
are distributed.

00:52:43.090 --> 00:52:46.720
That A might have an abundance
of feature vectors conferring

00:52:46.720 --> 00:52:48.672
high outcomes in the
screening process.

00:52:48.672 --> 00:52:51.130
And D might have an abundance
of feature vectors conferring

00:52:51.130 --> 00:52:52.930
lower outcomes.

00:52:52.930 --> 00:52:56.680
So in particular, we could
compute the average value of F

00:52:56.680 --> 00:52:58.630
in both group A group D.

00:52:58.630 --> 00:53:02.000
Let's call that VA
of F and VD of F.

00:53:02.000 --> 00:53:05.570
And we could look at now
what sociologists might call

00:53:05.570 --> 00:53:07.640
the structural disadvantage.

00:53:07.640 --> 00:53:11.420
Namely, D of F is now the
difference in averages.

00:53:11.420 --> 00:53:13.700
It's the difference between
the average value in A

00:53:13.700 --> 00:53:16.460
and the average value in D.

00:53:16.460 --> 00:53:19.128
Now this is something
which precedes

00:53:19.128 --> 00:53:20.420
the introduction of the output.

00:53:20.420 --> 00:53:22.650
This was already
present in society.

00:53:22.650 --> 00:53:24.740
And so while we might
want to use the algorithm

00:53:24.740 --> 00:53:27.080
to remediate that, it's
important to realize

00:53:27.080 --> 00:53:30.250
that is something that
predates the algorithm,

00:53:30.250 --> 00:53:32.750
it's not a case, not a question
about what the algorithm has

00:53:32.750 --> 00:53:34.228
added to the process.

00:53:34.228 --> 00:53:36.020
Now the algorithm will
morph this function.

00:53:36.020 --> 00:53:40.040
And so for any
function, not just F,

00:53:40.040 --> 00:53:42.380
we might end up being
interested in disparity.

00:53:42.380 --> 00:53:46.040
Let's call that D of W. So
the difference in averages

00:53:46.040 --> 00:53:47.820
for any function [INAUDIBLE].

00:53:47.820 --> 00:53:52.080
Now, let's think about the ways
in which the algorithm changes

00:53:52.080 --> 00:53:53.780
this function as it goes.

00:53:53.780 --> 00:53:55.640
So first of all, the
algorithm designer

00:53:55.640 --> 00:53:57.705
is going to use a
function G instead of F,

00:53:57.705 --> 00:53:59.580
because the real F is
not accessible to them.

00:53:59.580 --> 00:54:01.100
So they're going to
use an approximation.

00:54:01.100 --> 00:54:02.780
Second, the full
feature of vector X

00:54:02.780 --> 00:54:04.130
is not accessible to them.

00:54:04.130 --> 00:54:09.080
So they're going to use R and
R of X instead of the full X.

00:54:09.080 --> 00:54:11.900
And finally, because
R of X is much shorter

00:54:11.900 --> 00:54:14.660
than X, they're going to
use a transformed objective,

00:54:14.660 --> 00:54:18.898
some shortened function H
composed with R instead of G.

00:54:18.898 --> 00:54:21.190
And finally, there's going
be a training procedure that

00:54:21.190 --> 00:54:24.340
produces a function
T instead of H.

00:54:24.340 --> 00:54:27.790
So at the beginning, we had D of
F, the structural disadvantage.

00:54:27.790 --> 00:54:31.270
But at the end of this
pipeline, we have D of T,

00:54:31.270 --> 00:54:33.070
composed with R,
trained function

00:54:33.070 --> 00:54:35.210
on the reduced representation.

00:54:35.210 --> 00:54:37.570
And now, the final point
is that we actually

00:54:37.570 --> 00:54:41.440
see that we can take this
disparity, the disparity in T

00:54:41.440 --> 00:54:43.960
composed with R produced by
the machine learning algorithm

00:54:43.960 --> 00:54:46.360
and break it into
these four components.

00:54:46.360 --> 00:54:50.500
The structural disadvantage
that preceded the algorithm's

00:54:50.500 --> 00:54:53.890
introduction,
followed by the bias

00:54:53.890 --> 00:54:56.530
from the choice of outcome,
G instead of F. The bias

00:54:56.530 --> 00:54:58.450
from the choice of
features, reduced

00:54:58.450 --> 00:55:01.040
representation R
of X instead of X.

00:55:01.040 --> 00:55:03.190
And finally, the bias from
the training procedure,

00:55:03.190 --> 00:55:06.750
T instead of the true
approximate function H.

00:55:06.750 --> 00:55:09.280
And so it actually says we
can, once we have access

00:55:09.280 --> 00:55:12.270
to the pipeline, we can
actually do this decomposition.

00:55:12.270 --> 00:55:14.370
And with that, I
wanted to wrap up.

00:55:14.370 --> 00:55:16.920
Again, algorithms
are presenting us

00:55:16.920 --> 00:55:20.430
with an interesting
opportunity, right?

00:55:20.430 --> 00:55:21.930
Human decision
making contains bias.

00:55:21.930 --> 00:55:24.340
As we've seen algorithmics
decision making contains bias.

00:55:24.340 --> 00:55:26.820
But there's a certain
kind of detectability

00:55:26.820 --> 00:55:28.680
of bias in algorthimic
decision making

00:55:28.680 --> 00:55:30.720
that's really unprecedented
if we compare it

00:55:30.720 --> 00:55:32.260
to human decision making.

00:55:32.260 --> 00:55:34.530
That they contain
explicit ingredients

00:55:34.530 --> 00:55:37.080
that allow us to attribute
components of the decision

00:55:37.080 --> 00:55:40.630
to aspects of the
screening process.

00:55:40.630 --> 00:55:43.890
This will only happen
with regulations in place

00:55:43.890 --> 00:55:46.320
that allow us to examine the
machine learning pipeline.

00:55:46.320 --> 00:55:49.033
But with that in place, it
really creates very interesting

00:55:49.033 --> 00:55:50.200
opportunities going forward.

00:55:50.200 --> 00:55:52.410
And one in which many fields are
going to have to play a part.

00:55:52.410 --> 00:55:53.130
Thanks very much.

00:55:56.140 --> 00:56:00.140
Thank you so much, Jon, for
the really insightful talk.

00:56:00.140 --> 00:56:02.230
And one of the
things that struck me

00:56:02.230 --> 00:56:07.150
was just the parallel use of
the term social technical gap,

00:56:07.150 --> 00:56:10.510
from the intro and of
course from your talk here.

00:56:10.510 --> 00:56:13.780
And one of the things
I love about your work

00:56:13.780 --> 00:56:18.310
is that you're looking deeply
at the socio, the social side,

00:56:18.310 --> 00:56:21.370
as well as investigating
particular formalisms that

00:56:21.370 --> 00:56:25.150
can be deployed to either
explain or implement systems.

00:56:25.150 --> 00:56:27.910
And so following that
line, one question

00:56:27.910 --> 00:56:29.620
that I have relating
to your work

00:56:29.620 --> 00:56:34.030
is that in your paper
on algorithmic fairness

00:56:34.030 --> 00:56:37.030
that relates to a lot of
the topics you spoke about,

00:56:37.030 --> 00:56:41.140
I was really struck by
your central argument which

00:56:41.140 --> 00:56:46.240
is that the strategy of
blinding an algorithm to race

00:56:46.240 --> 00:56:49.180
inadvertently detracts
from fairness.

00:56:49.180 --> 00:56:52.660
And what that really reminds
me on the social side

00:56:52.660 --> 00:56:55.570
is of Eduardo
[? Bunyah ?] Silva's work

00:56:55.570 --> 00:56:58.540
on colorblind racism,
in which he describes

00:56:58.540 --> 00:57:02.650
certain kind of frameworks that
relate to colorblind ideology,

00:57:02.650 --> 00:57:04.990
and how it not only
detracts from fairness,

00:57:04.990 --> 00:57:06.220
but actually discriminates.

00:57:06.220 --> 00:57:09.100
So for him, that's
abstract liberalism,

00:57:09.100 --> 00:57:12.580
naturalizing racist outcomes
like redlining, cultural

00:57:12.580 --> 00:57:14.430
racism, minimizing of racism.

00:57:14.430 --> 00:57:15.940
And so I just
wonder if you could

00:57:15.940 --> 00:57:21.790
help to build a bridge from
your comments and your insights

00:57:21.790 --> 00:57:25.120
about the limitations of
blinding algorithms to race

00:57:25.120 --> 00:57:29.170
and the notion of
colorblind racism

00:57:29.170 --> 00:57:32.980
and the way that might actually
implement particular types

00:57:32.980 --> 00:57:35.600
of discrimination.

00:57:35.600 --> 00:57:37.590
Yeah, thanks very much
for that question.

00:57:37.590 --> 00:57:39.560
And I think it's
the kind of question

00:57:39.560 --> 00:57:44.000
that I certainly look forward
to hearing the other panelists'

00:57:44.000 --> 00:57:47.660
thoughts on as well, because I
think you definitely appreciate

00:57:47.660 --> 00:57:49.940
being in this kind of
a conversation with so

00:57:49.940 --> 00:57:53.390
many perspectives represented.

00:57:53.390 --> 00:57:55.190
I think from the
computer science side,

00:57:55.190 --> 00:58:04.040
I think we always need to be
careful about the ways in which

00:58:04.040 --> 00:58:09.170
these issues of bias, for
example, come in through things

00:58:09.170 --> 00:58:12.887
that we construct that we often
think of as part of the given

00:58:12.887 --> 00:58:13.970
statement of the question.

00:58:13.970 --> 00:58:16.553
So I thought your example at the
very beginning of the session

00:58:16.553 --> 00:58:21.050
on recommender systems,
is one example of this.

00:58:21.050 --> 00:58:24.830
I think any time that we
set up a ranking of people

00:58:24.830 --> 00:58:27.050
or a clustering
of people, we have

00:58:27.050 --> 00:58:28.490
to choose some
kind of objective.

00:58:28.490 --> 00:58:30.500
What does it mean for
people to be similar?

00:58:30.500 --> 00:58:33.048
And we often say we start
from this similarity metric

00:58:33.048 --> 00:58:35.090
and then we put a lot of
work into the clustering

00:58:35.090 --> 00:58:36.150
or the ranking.

00:58:36.150 --> 00:58:40.957
But of course, obviously, a
huge and often untold number

00:58:40.957 --> 00:58:43.040
of unstated assumptions
went into the construction

00:58:43.040 --> 00:58:45.200
of that objective
function that we

00:58:45.200 --> 00:58:51.280
take as given in the
statement of the question.

00:58:51.280 --> 00:58:56.570
And so I think that's one
dimension of the gap that I

00:58:56.570 --> 00:58:58.820
think we're all thinking
about, that there's been this

00:58:58.820 --> 00:59:03.830
territory that, somehow, I think
each side of the discussion has

00:59:03.830 --> 00:59:06.740
sometimes assumed that perhaps
the other one was somehow

00:59:06.740 --> 00:59:08.810
trying to get under
control, and in fact wasn't.

00:59:08.810 --> 00:59:11.688
That on the computer
science side,

00:59:11.688 --> 00:59:13.730
we'll sometimes take the
problem formulation as a

00:59:13.730 --> 00:59:15.350
given at some low level.

00:59:15.350 --> 00:59:18.120
And then we'll work very hard
on extending it from there.

00:59:18.120 --> 00:59:20.120
But of course, a lot of
that represented choices

00:59:20.120 --> 00:59:23.750
that I think
incorporate the bias.

00:59:23.750 --> 00:59:26.750
I think the same thing
happens again thinking

00:59:26.750 --> 00:59:28.500
about the connection
to some of the things

00:59:28.500 --> 00:59:31.700
you were saying in the
construction of features.

00:59:31.700 --> 00:59:35.912
All of these systems, no system
is dealing with the person,

00:59:35.912 --> 00:59:37.370
every algorithmic
system is dealing

00:59:37.370 --> 00:59:38.840
with the representation.

00:59:38.840 --> 00:59:42.170
And so I think the
choice of encodings

00:59:42.170 --> 00:59:44.240
and the choice of
representation, which is again

00:59:44.240 --> 00:59:47.570
I think very much in the theme
of some of the events going on

00:59:47.570 --> 00:59:50.960
here is again
something that often

00:59:50.960 --> 00:59:53.692
in the modeling of the problem,
people take that is given.

00:59:53.692 --> 00:59:55.400
They say I'm going to
start from the fact

00:59:55.400 --> 00:59:57.290
that you've represented
individuals.

00:59:57.290 --> 00:59:59.720
You've represented applicants,
you've represented users.

00:59:59.720 --> 01:00:01.490
You've represented
readers of the news.

01:00:01.490 --> 01:00:04.760
All these things using a
certain collection of features

01:00:04.760 --> 01:00:06.500
or at least some
kind of formalism.

01:00:06.500 --> 01:00:08.730
And then I'll do the
best I can from there.

01:00:08.730 --> 01:00:10.580
And so I think a
lot of the question

01:00:10.580 --> 01:00:13.760
is to actually ask, where did
that representation come from?

01:00:13.760 --> 01:00:17.140
What does that encode?

01:00:17.140 --> 01:00:19.036
It makes sense.

01:00:19.036 --> 01:00:22.030
I think it's such
a fascinating area

01:00:22.030 --> 01:00:24.550
and we don't have a
lot of time before we

01:00:24.550 --> 01:00:27.520
go to our next speaker.

01:00:27.520 --> 01:00:31.210
But maybe just us planting
food for thought for later.

01:00:31.210 --> 01:00:35.470
I feel like one of the
central dialogues we can--

01:00:35.470 --> 01:00:37.660
points of dialogue we could
have within this panel

01:00:37.660 --> 01:00:41.620
is about the essential
assumptions within each field.

01:00:41.620 --> 01:00:44.380
And that sometimes
there is a sense--

01:00:44.380 --> 01:00:48.280
so coming from computer
science myself as well,

01:00:48.280 --> 01:00:49.960
and there can be a
sense that if you're

01:00:49.960 --> 01:00:53.500
using algorithmic or
formless approaches,

01:00:53.500 --> 01:00:56.140
then you're taking a
reductionistic approach

01:00:56.140 --> 01:00:57.050
to society.

01:00:57.050 --> 01:00:58.600
And I think what
your work shows is

01:00:58.600 --> 01:01:02.470
that you can use formalisms
to address subjective issues

01:01:02.470 --> 01:01:06.560
in ways that really provide
additional insight that

01:01:06.560 --> 01:01:08.560
can be useful for those
subjective social issues

01:01:08.560 --> 01:01:09.060
as well.

01:01:09.060 --> 01:01:11.380
So I hope we can pick up
the point about the role

01:01:11.380 --> 01:01:14.290
of formalization and
how it's not always

01:01:14.290 --> 01:01:17.780
reductive and work such
as [INAUDIBLE] later.

01:01:17.780 --> 01:01:18.280
Thanks.

01:01:18.280 --> 01:01:18.880
[INTERPOSING VOICES]

01:01:18.880 --> 01:01:19.450
So--

01:01:19.450 --> 01:01:21.380
Looking forward to that.

01:01:21.380 --> 01:01:22.240
Thanks so much.

01:01:22.240 --> 01:01:26.380
And so next up, we
have Behnaz Farahi

01:01:26.380 --> 01:01:30.760
who will speak from a
different perspective and begin

01:01:30.760 --> 01:01:33.490
and her reported talk
will provide insights

01:01:33.490 --> 01:01:36.590
from an artistic point of
view, bringing together

01:01:36.590 --> 01:01:40.120
our trifecta of sociology,
computer science, and the arts

01:01:40.120 --> 01:01:42.400
in particular looking at
reflective and interpretive

01:01:42.400 --> 01:01:46.460
issues around these
issues of bias in AI.

01:01:46.460 --> 01:01:50.609
So let's begin
Behnaz's presentation.

01:02:36.520 --> 01:02:38.290
We live in an age
where we are immersed

01:02:38.290 --> 01:02:41.290
in the world of machine
learning algorithms, computer

01:02:41.290 --> 01:02:44.110
vision, and biometric sensors.

01:02:44.110 --> 01:02:46.125
But what does this all mean?

01:02:46.125 --> 01:02:49.510
Why these computational
systems not replicate somewhat

01:02:49.510 --> 01:02:52.750
disturbingly our
underlying biases

01:02:52.750 --> 01:02:55.900
and serve to reinforce the
marginalization of those who

01:02:55.900 --> 01:02:58.860
have typically been excluded?

01:02:58.860 --> 01:03:03.700
Is AI necessarily complicit in
replicating human prejudice?

01:03:03.700 --> 01:03:05.635
Or are there ways
of deploying AI

01:03:05.635 --> 01:03:08.860
as a mean of
exposing human bias?

01:03:08.860 --> 01:03:11.680
I would suggest that there
are potential strategies that

01:03:11.680 --> 01:03:17.300
could be deployed for AI
to actually overcome bias.

01:03:17.300 --> 01:03:20.900
In this presentation, I'm going
to address notions of the gaze,

01:03:20.900 --> 01:03:23.780
surveillance, and ways of
seeing through the lens

01:03:23.780 --> 01:03:26.360
of my own creative practice.

01:03:26.360 --> 01:03:29.030
By implementing computer
vision technologies,

01:03:29.030 --> 01:03:32.870
I hope to examine how different
strategies of the gaze

01:03:32.870 --> 01:03:36.470
can be seen to undermine
various form of power structure

01:03:36.470 --> 01:03:41.510
and to promote
forms of resistance.

01:03:41.510 --> 01:03:43.070
Here I'm trying
to avoid any form

01:03:43.070 --> 01:03:44.900
of technological determinism.

01:03:44.900 --> 01:03:48.170
Rather than rehashing arguments
as to whether surveillance

01:03:48.170 --> 01:03:51.770
is good or bad, I hope to take
an intersectional feminist

01:03:51.770 --> 01:03:55.970
approach to show what constitute
the gaze and surveillance.

01:03:55.970 --> 01:03:58.940
And to consider what the
strategies of resistance

01:03:58.940 --> 01:04:02.660
might be prove to be effective
in art and design practices.

01:04:07.080 --> 01:04:10.950
In my work, I have used various
computer vision technologies

01:04:10.950 --> 01:04:13.360
to enhance human perception.

01:04:13.360 --> 01:04:15.990
For example, this is
an emotive wearable

01:04:15.990 --> 01:04:18.870
which can recognize and respond
to the facial expressions

01:04:18.870 --> 01:04:22.920
of people around, the
different type of behaviors.

01:04:22.920 --> 01:04:26.190
This type of smart,
soft, robotic garment

01:04:26.190 --> 01:04:29.130
could potentially
benefit those with autism

01:04:29.130 --> 01:04:32.280
who have difficulties
recognizing facial expressions.

01:05:18.460 --> 01:05:21.820
Another work, [INAUDIBLE],,
is an emotive collar

01:05:21.820 --> 01:05:25.870
equipped with a facial tracking
camera, an array of rotating

01:05:25.870 --> 01:05:28.780
quills, which respond to
the movement of onlookers

01:05:28.780 --> 01:05:30.850
and their facial expressions.

01:05:30.850 --> 01:05:32.860
Similar to how a
blind person could

01:05:32.860 --> 01:05:35.470
sense through
haptic information,

01:05:35.470 --> 01:05:39.400
the movement of the wearable
would allow the wearer to sense

01:05:39.400 --> 01:05:42.100
where people were standing
and the emotions they're

01:05:42.100 --> 01:05:44.245
expressing even with
their eyes closed.

01:06:06.810 --> 01:06:09.390
Here, I hope to address
two questions related

01:06:09.390 --> 01:06:11.310
to the notion of the gaze.

01:06:11.310 --> 01:06:14.250
Firstly, what does it
mean to be observed?

01:06:14.250 --> 01:06:18.930
Secondly, what do we see when
we look out at the world?

01:06:18.930 --> 01:06:22.410
Through my argument, I use
gaze as an interchangeable

01:06:22.410 --> 01:06:28.110
concept, which could be shared
between human and machine.

01:06:28.110 --> 01:06:31.140
This take us to the historical
concept of panopticon.

01:06:31.140 --> 01:06:34.500
The panopticon refers to a
type of institutional building

01:06:34.500 --> 01:06:36.510
and a system of
control envisioned

01:06:36.510 --> 01:06:41.040
by English philosopher, Jeremy
Bentham in 18th century.

01:06:41.040 --> 01:06:43.680
The layout of the
panopticon allows a guard

01:06:43.680 --> 01:06:47.398
to observe occupants without
them knowing whether or not

01:06:47.398 --> 01:06:48.315
they're being watched.

01:06:53.590 --> 01:06:55.570
French philosopher,
Michel Foucault,

01:06:55.570 --> 01:06:59.830
revitalizes interest in
the panopticon in his 1975

01:06:59.830 --> 01:07:01.840
book, Discipline and Punish.

01:07:01.840 --> 01:07:04.480
And uses it to
illustrate such a model

01:07:04.480 --> 01:07:07.180
could be applied to
disciplinary societies

01:07:07.180 --> 01:07:09.670
in order to control
their citizens.

01:07:09.670 --> 01:07:11.980
He described a prisoner
of a panopticon

01:07:11.980 --> 01:07:17.200
as being at the receiving end
of asymmetrical surveillance.

01:07:17.200 --> 01:07:20.530
As a consequence, the
inmate polices himself

01:07:20.530 --> 01:07:23.440
for fear of punishment.

01:07:23.440 --> 01:07:27.430
What is fascinating about this
example is that rather than

01:07:27.430 --> 01:07:30.640
external actions, the
gaze of the watcher

01:07:30.640 --> 01:07:32.890
is internalized
to such an extent

01:07:32.890 --> 01:07:36.655
that each prisoner become
his or her own watch person.

01:07:39.310 --> 01:07:42.430
Similarly, we could
argue that this

01:07:42.430 --> 01:07:46.600
is what allows rules and
regulation to be internalized

01:07:46.600 --> 01:07:50.090
so as to inform our
actions, behaviors,

01:07:50.090 --> 01:07:51.385
and even our beliefs.

01:07:54.610 --> 01:07:57.940
Moreover, the manner in which
we naturalize and internalize

01:07:57.940 --> 01:08:00.790
rules, it could be
claimed cause a society

01:08:00.790 --> 01:08:04.030
to be less willing to
contest unjust laws,

01:08:04.030 --> 01:08:07.420
and accept a dominant outlook.

01:08:07.420 --> 01:08:11.290
Similar to how the inmates
are not aware whether or not

01:08:11.290 --> 01:08:13.570
they're being watched,
we are not aware

01:08:13.570 --> 01:08:15.130
that we have been
controlled through

01:08:15.130 --> 01:08:18.609
naturalized dominant rules
rooted in our own culture.

01:08:25.380 --> 01:08:28.890
This internalized
asymmetrical power structure

01:08:28.890 --> 01:08:31.680
could be seen in the
notion of male gaze.

01:08:31.680 --> 01:08:34.620
In her essay on Visual
Pleasure and Narrative Cinema,

01:08:34.620 --> 01:08:37.229
Laura Mulvey exposes
the asymmetry

01:08:37.229 --> 01:08:39.689
of social and political
power relations

01:08:39.689 --> 01:08:41.939
between men and women.

01:08:41.939 --> 01:08:43.770
Mulvey claims that
the male gaze serves

01:08:43.770 --> 01:08:46.740
to depict women as
the object of pleasure

01:08:46.740 --> 01:08:48.530
for the heterosexual
male viewer.

01:08:51.240 --> 01:08:53.910
Besides the fact that
women are regularly

01:08:53.910 --> 01:08:56.700
subject to sexual harassment
whenever they enter

01:08:56.700 --> 01:09:00.540
public space through various
forms of looked-edness

01:09:00.540 --> 01:09:03.720
women have absorbed
all this unconsciously

01:09:03.720 --> 01:09:06.044
as a form of
internalized male gaze.

01:09:08.600 --> 01:09:10.700
What if women were
to subvert this

01:09:10.700 --> 01:09:12.440
through the power of their gaze?

01:09:26.420 --> 01:09:29.340
Could we draw up in
computer vision technologies

01:09:29.340 --> 01:09:35.010
to allow women to know when
onlookers are staring at them?

01:09:35.010 --> 01:09:37.169
Augmented with facial
and gaze tracking

01:09:37.169 --> 01:09:39.569
technology in smart
materials, this cape

01:09:39.569 --> 01:09:42.240
responds to the onlookers' gaze.

01:09:42.240 --> 01:09:45.450
The project engages with
broader social issues

01:09:45.450 --> 01:09:49.229
such as the male gaze
on the female body.

01:09:49.229 --> 01:09:51.390
The facial tracking
algorithm in this piece

01:09:51.390 --> 01:09:56.100
detects age, gender, and
gaze of the onlooker.

01:09:56.100 --> 01:09:58.650
While we know that
gender is performative

01:09:58.650 --> 01:10:01.410
and it doesn't depend
on pure representation,

01:10:01.410 --> 01:10:04.680
the movement of the garment
based on the viewer's gaze

01:10:04.680 --> 01:10:08.040
could unfold in new
sets of social meanings.

01:10:08.040 --> 01:10:10.710
If you are the wearer, you
know which parts of your body

01:10:10.710 --> 01:10:12.040
is being looked at.

01:10:12.040 --> 01:10:15.090
And if you are an onlooker,
you know that your action

01:10:15.090 --> 01:10:17.130
has been noticed.

01:10:17.130 --> 01:10:20.550
The aim is to see how
different strategies could

01:10:20.550 --> 01:10:23.550
be used to undermine
the patriarchal system

01:10:23.550 --> 01:10:27.330
by developing form of resistance
using surveillance technology.

01:10:32.510 --> 01:10:35.390
I wonder what do we see as
we gaze out to the world?

01:10:51.260 --> 01:10:55.220
As a thought experiment, French
psychoanalysis, Jacques Lacan,

01:10:55.220 --> 01:10:59.390
invites us to envision ourselves
before a female mantis.

01:10:59.390 --> 01:11:02.780
The female mantis is known
for devouring his partner

01:11:02.780 --> 01:11:04.280
after sex.

01:11:04.280 --> 01:11:07.010
In Lacan's experiments,
we're wearing a mask

01:11:07.010 --> 01:11:10.310
and we do not know whether
the mask represent us

01:11:10.310 --> 01:11:12.290
as a female or male.

01:11:12.290 --> 01:11:14.810
And therefore, we don't
know whether or not

01:11:14.810 --> 01:11:18.290
we will be eaten by
the female mantis.

01:11:18.290 --> 01:11:21.750
We simply do not know
what we are to the other.

01:11:21.750 --> 01:11:25.460
And we often project our
own very biases to the other

01:11:25.460 --> 01:11:28.860
in order to
understand the other.

01:11:28.860 --> 01:11:31.340
In fact, we don't see
anyone as they are.

01:11:31.340 --> 01:11:33.440
Rather we see them as we are.

01:11:40.180 --> 01:11:43.150
And how we are is
informed by the conformity

01:11:43.150 --> 01:11:45.880
to many of behavioral
assumptions dictated

01:11:45.880 --> 01:11:48.710
by laws and customs.

01:11:48.710 --> 01:11:51.850
Although the colonial
racist and sexist gaze

01:11:51.850 --> 01:11:56.140
might project oppression,
backwardness, and terrorism

01:11:56.140 --> 01:12:00.550
onto the image of Muslim
woman wearing a burka,

01:12:00.550 --> 01:12:02.890
I would like to stress
the inherent difference

01:12:02.890 --> 01:12:05.560
between a mask and masquerade.

01:12:05.560 --> 01:12:09.410
While the mask hides,
the masquerade reveals.

01:12:09.410 --> 01:12:12.520
But perhaps we could
suggest that both

01:12:12.520 --> 01:12:16.235
are acting to some extent as
a shield against the intrusive

01:12:16.235 --> 01:12:16.735
gaze.

01:12:33.550 --> 01:12:36.220
This project is inspired
by the historical mask

01:12:36.220 --> 01:12:39.990
worn by Bhandari
women from South Iran.

01:12:39.990 --> 01:12:42.430
Has been said that this
mask were developed

01:12:42.430 --> 01:12:45.910
during Portuguese colonial
rule as a way of protecting

01:12:45.910 --> 01:12:49.300
the wearer from the gaze
of a slave master looking

01:12:49.300 --> 01:12:50.565
for pretty women.

01:12:53.600 --> 01:12:56.240
From these Iranian
masks protecting women

01:12:56.240 --> 01:13:00.260
from the patriarchal oppression,
to mask off Northwest Coast

01:13:00.260 --> 01:13:04.490
Indians, to mask worn
as political statements,

01:13:04.490 --> 01:13:08.450
mask take on a range of
different religious, social and

01:13:08.450 --> 01:13:09.950
political roles.

01:13:09.950 --> 01:13:12.710
And offer an interesting
insight into how

01:13:12.710 --> 01:13:17.000
we could use them to
either conceal or reveal

01:13:17.000 --> 01:13:20.090
our identities.

01:13:20.090 --> 01:13:22.730
While these Iranian
masks cover most

01:13:22.730 --> 01:13:25.070
of the wearer's
face except the eye,

01:13:25.070 --> 01:13:28.730
I've been wondering how the eyes
could be used as an expressive

01:13:28.730 --> 01:13:32.090
medium for communication.

01:13:32.090 --> 01:13:35.300
In my research, I came across
an interesting incident

01:13:35.300 --> 01:13:38.180
in which an American
officer uses blinking

01:13:38.180 --> 01:13:40.160
to send a secret message.

01:13:40.160 --> 01:13:43.220
In this video, you're seeing
Admiral Jeremiah Denton

01:13:43.220 --> 01:13:48.020
blinking the word torture using
Morse code during his captivity

01:13:48.020 --> 01:13:48.806
in the Vietnam.

01:13:55.520 --> 01:13:58.024
Do you think that [INAUDIBLE]?

01:13:58.024 --> 01:14:02.360
No, I don't know
what is happening.

01:14:02.360 --> 01:14:05.810
But whatever the position
of my government is,

01:14:05.810 --> 01:14:08.420
I support it, fully.

01:14:08.420 --> 01:14:10.670
Whatever the position
of my government is,

01:14:10.670 --> 01:14:12.050
I believe in it, yes sir.

01:14:12.050 --> 01:14:13.730
I am a member of
that government.

01:14:13.730 --> 01:14:16.850
The use of code for
communication is nothing new.

01:14:16.850 --> 01:14:20.570
And in fact, women came
up with a code word

01:14:20.570 --> 01:14:23.000
has a method for
reporting domestic abuse

01:14:23.000 --> 01:14:23.975
during this lockdown.

01:14:29.150 --> 01:14:31.340
In this project,
I was particularly

01:14:31.340 --> 01:14:34.670
inspired by a recent
experiment involving two AI

01:14:34.670 --> 01:14:37.160
bots at the Facebook AI lab.

01:14:37.160 --> 01:14:39.830
The intention was to
monitor how two bots might

01:14:39.830 --> 01:14:41.750
develop a dialogue.

01:14:41.750 --> 01:14:45.200
However, during this
experiment, a bot seemingly

01:14:45.200 --> 01:14:49.100
started to formulate their
own language, a language

01:14:49.100 --> 01:14:51.380
that no human could understand.

01:14:51.380 --> 01:14:55.610
The researchers had to intervene
and tweak the algorithms

01:14:55.610 --> 01:14:58.230
to prevent this from happening.

01:14:58.230 --> 01:15:02.120
The story could also reveal
how knowledge is power,

01:15:02.120 --> 01:15:04.640
and an ability to
understand unnerves

01:15:04.640 --> 01:15:07.090
those who wish to
maintain their authority.

01:15:09.880 --> 01:15:11.860
For the source text
of this project,

01:15:11.860 --> 01:15:15.100
I use the seminal article,
Can the Subaltern Speak?

01:15:15.100 --> 01:15:18.430
by the feminist
theorist, Gayatri Spivak.

01:15:18.430 --> 01:15:21.700
She asked whether it might
be possible for the colonies

01:15:21.700 --> 01:15:24.550
or the subaltern to
have a voice in the face

01:15:24.550 --> 01:15:27.970
of colonial oppression?

01:15:27.970 --> 01:15:30.040
Using machine
learning algorithm,

01:15:30.040 --> 01:15:34.090
we can use Markov
chain to create a text.

01:15:34.090 --> 01:15:38.770
We represent each words as
a state and view transitions

01:15:38.770 --> 01:15:41.350
as the likelihood
that the specific word

01:15:41.350 --> 01:15:43.930
might go after another.

01:15:43.930 --> 01:15:47.680
We can consider these states
either a word or character

01:15:47.680 --> 01:15:48.550
level.

01:15:48.550 --> 01:15:50.830
And we can analyze
what the probability

01:15:50.830 --> 01:15:55.090
might be of a given letter
appearing after three letters.

01:15:55.090 --> 01:15:58.030
The engrams of a given
text provides a strategy

01:15:58.030 --> 01:16:00.310
for generating the next text.

01:16:00.310 --> 01:16:03.250
The Markov chain could
be therefore used

01:16:03.250 --> 01:16:06.910
to generate text where
each new letters is

01:16:06.910 --> 01:16:10.870
dependent upon the
previous letters.

01:16:10.870 --> 01:16:13.870
As the mass communicates
using BLE protocol,

01:16:13.870 --> 01:16:19.660
the algorithm is repeated
and the engrams changes.

01:16:19.660 --> 01:16:22.060
The smaller the engram
gets, the less meaning

01:16:22.060 --> 01:16:24.460
there is in the
generated text from

01:16:24.460 --> 01:16:27.400
the linguistic perspective.

01:16:27.400 --> 01:16:30.100
This letter generated
through this process

01:16:30.100 --> 01:16:33.070
is translated into Morse
code, which informs

01:16:33.070 --> 01:16:35.650
the movement of the actuators.

01:16:35.650 --> 01:16:38.080
In the video documentation
of this project, which

01:16:38.080 --> 01:16:40.240
will be shown
shortly, you will see

01:16:40.240 --> 01:16:44.200
two women wearing masks, covered
with eyelashes controlled

01:16:44.200 --> 01:16:45.460
by AI.

01:16:45.460 --> 01:16:47.440
They begin to communicate
with each other,

01:16:47.440 --> 01:16:50.650
blinking their eyelashes
in rapid succession.

01:16:50.650 --> 01:16:52.900
The aim is to develop
a secret message

01:16:52.900 --> 01:16:57.490
for transmitting information
between multiple women.

01:16:57.490 --> 01:17:00.670
The project brings together
design, critical thinking,

01:17:00.670 --> 01:17:01.990
feminism, and AI.

01:18:22.030 --> 01:18:24.550
Winston Churchill once
said, "We shape our tools,

01:18:24.550 --> 01:18:26.890
and thereafter our
tools shape us."

01:18:26.890 --> 01:18:31.510
But doesn't this suggest a form
of technological determinism?

01:18:31.510 --> 01:18:35.500
After all, there is no inherent
application for any tool.

01:18:35.500 --> 01:18:38.140
The knife could be
used as a murder weapon

01:18:38.140 --> 01:18:41.680
or just as a tool
for cutting an apple.

01:18:41.680 --> 01:18:44.980
Similar arguments could be
made about surveillance.

01:18:44.980 --> 01:18:48.610
The question we should
ask are, in what context?

01:18:48.610 --> 01:18:52.210
How and for whom
are we using it?

01:18:52.210 --> 01:18:55.270
We should be aware of
potential problems with AI.

01:18:55.270 --> 01:18:58.630
For once an issue is recognized
as problem that becomes

01:18:58.630 --> 01:19:00.490
a different kind of problem.

01:19:00.490 --> 01:19:03.580
It becomes a problem, not
by which we are trapped,

01:19:03.580 --> 01:19:07.310
but rather with
which we can deal.

01:19:07.310 --> 01:19:09.400
Bias is certainly
a problem which

01:19:09.400 --> 01:19:12.460
originates from human
beings and is replicated

01:19:12.460 --> 01:19:16.060
and even exacerbated in AI.

01:19:16.060 --> 01:19:18.100
As we are training the
artificial algorithms

01:19:18.100 --> 01:19:21.790
to learn to see the world, it
is important to ask ourselves

01:19:21.790 --> 01:19:24.820
what does it mean to
be seen by the machine?

01:19:24.820 --> 01:19:27.490
And through the lens
of what kind of data

01:19:27.490 --> 01:19:29.935
are they trying to
look out to the world?

01:19:40.550 --> 01:19:44.510
So Thanks so much, Behnaz for
the fascinating, creative,

01:19:44.510 --> 01:19:45.560
and evocative talk.

01:19:45.560 --> 01:19:51.290
And one of the things that your
talk brought to mind for me

01:19:51.290 --> 01:19:54.290
was that you're
looking both at issues

01:19:54.290 --> 01:19:57.780
that are implemented and
instantiated by the machine.

01:19:57.780 --> 01:20:01.130
So the gaze of the machine
that really surveillance

01:20:01.130 --> 01:20:04.730
technologies and so forth, as
well as the human phenomena

01:20:04.730 --> 01:20:07.310
that you're
discussing and putting

01:20:07.310 --> 01:20:10.070
into dialogue with critical
theory and the arts.

01:20:10.070 --> 01:20:12.170
And so I was struck
by one phrase

01:20:12.170 --> 01:20:15.170
early on in your talk
where you mentioned

01:20:15.170 --> 01:20:21.150
about resistivity strategies
against these kind of issues,

01:20:21.150 --> 01:20:24.860
whether it's a objectifying
gaze or other forms

01:20:24.860 --> 01:20:26.780
of discriminatory surveillance.

01:20:26.780 --> 01:20:30.440
And so you mentioned that
certain resistive strategies

01:20:30.440 --> 01:20:35.020
can be proved to be effective
in art and design practices.

01:20:35.020 --> 01:20:37.520
And so I wonder
just for you, what

01:20:37.520 --> 01:20:39.710
does it mean for a
resistive strategy

01:20:39.710 --> 01:20:43.250
to be proved effective in
art and design practice?

01:20:45.940 --> 01:20:48.270
Thank you so much for the
great question, folks.

01:20:48.270 --> 01:20:50.430
And also before I
answer, I want to say

01:20:50.430 --> 01:20:54.240
that this is a great honor to
be in such an inspiring line

01:20:54.240 --> 01:20:55.260
up of speakers.

01:20:55.260 --> 01:20:57.750
I'm honored to be here.

01:20:57.750 --> 01:21:02.640
To answer your question, I think
especially from art and design

01:21:02.640 --> 01:21:06.270
perspective, I'm
particularly interested

01:21:06.270 --> 01:21:10.740
in ways that we can use
various computational systems

01:21:10.740 --> 01:21:15.870
and repurpose those in order
to bring a new sort of insight

01:21:15.870 --> 01:21:21.390
or in a way subvert certain
dominant assumptions that we

01:21:21.390 --> 01:21:23.670
have, either through
our culture or

01:21:23.670 --> 01:21:29.770
through technological
assumptions that we have.

01:21:29.770 --> 01:21:35.250
So it's always this having
a hand on the technology

01:21:35.250 --> 01:21:40.170
and in order to
subvert it in a way.

01:21:40.170 --> 01:21:42.840
In particular, I would say
I'm really inspired also

01:21:42.840 --> 01:21:45.570
by the work of other artists,
such as Christoph [? Fadusco ?]

01:21:45.570 --> 01:21:49.500
and his notion of interrogative
design, in which we really

01:21:49.500 --> 01:21:52.800
look at design as a way
of addressing larger

01:21:52.800 --> 01:21:55.550
social, political,
cultural issues.

01:21:55.550 --> 01:21:57.810
And so in my case,
particularly I'm

01:21:57.810 --> 01:22:00.600
interested to use a
lot of computer vision

01:22:00.600 --> 01:22:04.440
technologies embedded in
the substrate of the matter,

01:22:04.440 --> 01:22:06.660
and see that how
materials could be

01:22:06.660 --> 01:22:08.940
intelligence and smart enough.

01:22:08.940 --> 01:22:11.910
But also to address
larger issues

01:22:11.910 --> 01:22:15.660
in order to empower
those that for instance

01:22:15.660 --> 01:22:19.500
in the latest project, the
women that they were oppressed

01:22:19.500 --> 01:22:25.800
or oppressed under
colonial oppression,

01:22:25.800 --> 01:22:29.860
how we can empower those
through the use of technology.

01:22:29.860 --> 01:22:35.730
So it's always bringing
technology and repurposing it,

01:22:35.730 --> 01:22:40.440
and give it a certain
twist for envisioning what

01:22:40.440 --> 01:22:41.760
the future would look like.

01:22:44.370 --> 01:22:45.870
Thanks so much for
the elaboration.

01:22:45.870 --> 01:22:49.170
And maybe if we continue
down the same path

01:22:49.170 --> 01:22:55.090
in the same phrase, there is
the particular word of proof.

01:22:55.090 --> 01:22:59.070
And so for a
researchers such as Jon,

01:22:59.070 --> 01:23:04.020
proof has a very
particular kind of meaning.

01:23:04.020 --> 01:23:06.690
And a researcher
such as Ruha might

01:23:06.690 --> 01:23:10.200
use a term such
as a demonstrate,

01:23:10.200 --> 01:23:13.330
depending on the kind of
work that she is doing.

01:23:13.330 --> 01:23:16.860
And so in work we are building
evocative objects maybe

01:23:16.860 --> 01:23:19.140
to spark critical reflection.

01:23:19.140 --> 01:23:22.170
Does proof mean that
something like you

01:23:22.170 --> 01:23:24.960
can demonstrate that
people think critically

01:23:24.960 --> 01:23:26.850
after engaging with this work?

01:23:26.850 --> 01:23:30.640
Or is there something else
that you're aiming for?

01:23:30.640 --> 01:23:32.340
So again, what does
it, in some sense,

01:23:32.340 --> 01:23:36.180
what does it mean to prove
within the domain in which you

01:23:36.180 --> 01:23:38.680
operate?

01:23:38.680 --> 01:23:41.770
I think a lot of time,
perhaps it's bringing

01:23:41.770 --> 01:23:43.370
a new type of questions.

01:23:43.370 --> 01:23:47.750
So it's bringing a new
sort of what if questions.

01:23:47.750 --> 01:23:52.450
What if women were
able to experience

01:23:52.450 --> 01:23:54.370
the gaze of others
on their as they're

01:23:54.370 --> 01:23:56.290
entering the public space?

01:23:56.290 --> 01:24:00.580
I think bringing this type
of provocative questions

01:24:00.580 --> 01:24:05.050
and creating conversations, and
sharing this work with others,

01:24:05.050 --> 01:24:12.580
and it creates I would say an
escape or a sort of imagination

01:24:12.580 --> 01:24:17.290
for how we can really reuse or
repurpose these technologies

01:24:17.290 --> 01:24:20.530
in a novel way that
reflect on critical issues

01:24:20.530 --> 01:24:24.670
on issues related either to
feminism or critical studies.

01:24:24.670 --> 01:24:29.050
And I'm really interested to see
how the world of design and art

01:24:29.050 --> 01:24:31.180
could learn from
those disciplines.

01:24:31.180 --> 01:24:34.210
And combine and merge
them together in order

01:24:34.210 --> 01:24:41.600
to empower those communities.

01:24:41.600 --> 01:24:45.170
Thanks, I really love this
idea that we're describing.

01:24:45.170 --> 01:24:48.260
The role of the arts
not to answer questions,

01:24:48.260 --> 01:24:49.952
but to provoke questions.

01:24:49.952 --> 01:24:51.410
And through those
questions, people

01:24:51.410 --> 01:24:53.660
can better understand the
operations of these kind

01:24:53.660 --> 01:24:55.940
of systems that impose bias.

01:24:55.940 --> 01:25:00.140
And to provoke those kind of
questions, then imagination,

01:25:00.140 --> 01:25:03.950
aesthetics, culture,
subjectivity,

01:25:03.950 --> 01:25:07.340
all of those kind of issues
within the realm of the work

01:25:07.340 --> 01:25:10.930
that you're doing become crucial
for that kind of practice.

01:25:10.930 --> 01:25:12.920
So I really appreciate
the kind of insights

01:25:12.920 --> 01:25:14.430
that you've brought.

01:25:14.430 --> 01:25:18.680
And it's also a kind
of segue because now we

01:25:18.680 --> 01:25:21.920
have all of the panelists
that together in some time

01:25:21.920 --> 01:25:26.550
to engage in
questions as a group.

01:25:26.550 --> 01:25:29.900
And so I would like just
to begin to transition

01:25:29.900 --> 01:25:32.790
into those questions.

01:25:32.790 --> 01:25:37.125
And maybe pick up with one
that came up earlier, again,

01:25:37.125 --> 01:25:38.750
my question with Jon
that he mentioned,

01:25:38.750 --> 01:25:42.140
maybe that would be a great
thing to talk about as a panel.

01:25:42.140 --> 01:25:45.830
And what the observation
was, it was this idea

01:25:45.830 --> 01:25:50.330
that in Jon's work,
he's showing that

01:25:50.330 --> 01:25:57.500
a particular type of a
system, if you, in that case,

01:25:57.500 --> 01:25:58.850
blind the algorithms to race.

01:25:58.850 --> 01:26:02.150
But if you blind it any
particular kind of social issue

01:26:02.150 --> 01:26:04.070
that relates to
discrimination, then you

01:26:04.070 --> 01:26:06.050
could inadvertently
detract from fairness.

01:26:06.050 --> 01:26:07.758
And then the other
thing that I mentioned

01:26:07.758 --> 01:26:12.590
was that in the social
sciences that the idea

01:26:12.590 --> 01:26:14.270
of colorblind
racism, which seems

01:26:14.270 --> 01:26:17.480
to have a very strong
parallel to this kind

01:26:17.480 --> 01:26:18.470
of algorithmic insight.

01:26:18.470 --> 01:26:23.360
And so maybe to begin, I could
ask Ruha the same question.

01:26:23.360 --> 01:26:25.550
So what is your
take on reconciling

01:26:25.550 --> 01:26:30.080
this idea that computationally
blinding algorithms from race

01:26:30.080 --> 01:26:32.720
detracts from fairness,
and then socially

01:26:32.720 --> 01:26:35.660
colorblindness also
detracts from fairness

01:26:35.660 --> 01:26:39.260
or even more encodes racism?

01:26:39.260 --> 01:26:41.150
So can you just
elaborate on what you see

01:26:41.150 --> 01:26:44.160
coming from that connection?

01:26:44.160 --> 01:26:46.190
Can you restate
the question a bit?

01:26:46.190 --> 01:26:54.130
It's a little hard for me to
get at what you're asking.

01:26:54.130 --> 01:26:55.110
Sure, I'd be happy to.

01:26:55.110 --> 01:26:57.580
Yes, so it's really
just reconciling,

01:26:57.580 --> 01:27:00.880
I think, a similar insight
coming from two points of view.

01:27:00.880 --> 01:27:03.970
And so in computer
science, the idea

01:27:03.970 --> 01:27:08.140
is that when you
look at fairness

01:27:08.140 --> 01:27:10.870
through the lens
of that estimation

01:27:10.870 --> 01:27:14.320
approaches, the
objective functions, even

01:27:14.320 --> 01:27:16.210
formal definitions.

01:27:16.210 --> 01:27:20.340
Within that domain,
then Jon has found

01:27:20.340 --> 01:27:23.620
that blinding an algorithm to
race detracts from fairness.

01:27:23.620 --> 01:27:27.610
And I know that that's also
an idea within sociology

01:27:27.610 --> 01:27:29.440
from another perspective.

01:27:29.440 --> 01:27:35.710
The idea that colorblindness
that is blinding or ignoring

01:27:35.710 --> 01:27:38.410
the role of race also
detracts from fairness.

01:27:38.410 --> 01:27:40.750
And so I'd ask if
you could elaborate

01:27:40.750 --> 01:27:43.660
on that from your lens as well.

01:27:43.660 --> 01:27:48.610
That kind of role of obscuring
an issue such as race

01:27:48.610 --> 01:27:53.870
and the way that, that can end
up detracting for fairness.

01:27:53.870 --> 01:27:56.120
Yeah I certainly can.

01:27:56.120 --> 01:28:01.070
So it is true and you cited
Bonilla-Silva and others

01:28:01.070 --> 01:28:05.660
where it just comes down
to this faulty notion

01:28:05.660 --> 01:28:09.080
that ignoring something
makes it go away.

01:28:09.080 --> 01:28:13.940
And really, it's just a way
of delaying thought or action

01:28:13.940 --> 01:28:14.540
around it.

01:28:14.540 --> 01:28:16.790
Now something I want
to put in conversation

01:28:16.790 --> 01:28:19.430
with this idea of
colorblind racism

01:28:19.430 --> 01:28:22.760
is that paying
attention to things,

01:28:22.760 --> 01:28:27.290
collecting data on
issues that oftentimes we

01:28:27.290 --> 01:28:32.090
and moving away from
ignoring, assume

01:28:32.090 --> 01:28:36.020
that simply producing knowledge,
or highlighting, or exposing

01:28:36.020 --> 01:28:39.590
certain issues to the use
of data and various tools,

01:28:39.590 --> 01:28:41.870
is itself a solution.

01:28:41.870 --> 01:28:45.170
And an idea that I write
a little bit about in Race

01:28:45.170 --> 01:28:49.490
After Technology is this
notion of the datafication

01:28:49.490 --> 01:28:51.020
of injustice.

01:28:51.020 --> 01:28:56.990
That is to say that we are often
asking questions about issues

01:28:56.990 --> 01:29:02.870
that we should or we could
already have the answers to.

01:29:02.870 --> 01:29:05.150
That is to say we're
producing more and more data

01:29:05.150 --> 01:29:07.820
and knowledge about
problems that we know

01:29:07.820 --> 01:29:11.420
exist, in some cases that
we know how to tackle,

01:29:11.420 --> 01:29:13.850
and that there's not the
social and political will

01:29:13.850 --> 01:29:15.150
to deal with.

01:29:15.150 --> 01:29:17.780
And so I want to put on the
table this idea that, yes,

01:29:17.780 --> 01:29:21.530
ignoring let's say racism
and other forms of inequality

01:29:21.530 --> 01:29:24.350
is certainly stands in
the way of social change

01:29:24.350 --> 01:29:25.760
and transformation.

01:29:25.760 --> 01:29:33.920
But sometimes too, the call for
more and more data and exposure

01:29:33.920 --> 01:29:37.790
of issues can also
be a delaying tactic

01:29:37.790 --> 01:29:40.500
to acting on what
we already know.

01:29:40.500 --> 01:29:43.040
And so that's a different
way of complicating

01:29:43.040 --> 01:29:45.590
the notion of paying
attention to issues

01:29:45.590 --> 01:29:47.570
is not a straightforward
solution.

01:29:47.570 --> 01:29:53.050
It can actually stand in the
way of making change as well.

01:29:53.050 --> 01:29:54.620
Thanks so much for the point.

01:29:54.620 --> 01:29:57.850
And I saw Jon
nodding as you speak.

01:29:57.850 --> 01:30:02.830
So could you maybe also
respond to Ruha's notion

01:30:02.830 --> 01:30:07.240
that sometimes data
collection or even big data

01:30:07.240 --> 01:30:09.670
could be seen as
a stalling tactic,

01:30:09.670 --> 01:30:13.720
or even just even if
not intentionally could

01:30:13.720 --> 01:30:15.100
have that kind of effect?

01:30:15.100 --> 01:30:19.000
And then also, it may be
related to your notion

01:30:19.000 --> 01:30:23.550
of algorithmic fairness to the
kind of sociological notion

01:30:23.550 --> 01:30:26.990
that Ruha has just described.

01:30:26.990 --> 01:30:28.490
Sure, yeah.

01:30:28.490 --> 01:30:32.210
Thanks for those points,
and thanks Ruha for the--

01:30:32.210 --> 01:30:34.400
I think those are
very important things

01:30:34.400 --> 01:30:38.810
to keep in mind that there
is this continuum where

01:30:38.810 --> 01:30:42.980
obviously not paying attention
to things poses these problems.

01:30:42.980 --> 01:30:47.480
But that there could be a
point where we have essentially

01:30:47.480 --> 01:30:49.655
what you said, we've
collected data,

01:30:49.655 --> 01:30:50.780
we're aware of the problem.

01:30:50.780 --> 01:30:53.300
And what we need next is
not more data collection

01:30:53.300 --> 01:30:56.360
or more intensive
analysis of that.

01:30:56.360 --> 01:30:59.300
I mean, a couple of things
that seem interesting

01:30:59.300 --> 01:31:02.670
here is one, the level of scale.

01:31:02.670 --> 01:31:04.880
So there's collecting data
about the aggregate nature

01:31:04.880 --> 01:31:05.840
of the problem.

01:31:05.840 --> 01:31:07.970
That we recognize
there's a policy change

01:31:07.970 --> 01:31:08.960
that needs to be made.

01:31:08.960 --> 01:31:10.440
We have ample evidence for that.

01:31:10.440 --> 01:31:11.857
And then there's
collecting nature

01:31:11.857 --> 01:31:14.630
about the individual problem in
a particular instance, right?

01:31:14.630 --> 01:31:17.210
So for example, if
a person believes

01:31:17.210 --> 01:31:19.910
that their particular
workplace is engaging

01:31:19.910 --> 01:31:23.513
in discriminatory behavior,
even if we have enormous data

01:31:23.513 --> 01:31:25.430
about the aggregate
effects of discrimination,

01:31:25.430 --> 01:31:27.180
we may not have much
data about the effect

01:31:27.180 --> 01:31:29.610
in their particular
place of work.

01:31:29.610 --> 01:31:32.660
That's a situation where
as these systems become

01:31:32.660 --> 01:31:35.030
more quantitative,
we have the potential

01:31:35.030 --> 01:31:38.270
to turn the argument from one
that might have been present

01:31:38.270 --> 01:31:40.160
four decades ago where
it now comes down

01:31:40.160 --> 01:31:44.030
to a qualitative estimation of
what the environment there was,

01:31:44.030 --> 01:31:45.290
what the decision making was.

01:31:45.290 --> 01:31:47.278
Where we may have much
more detailed traces

01:31:47.278 --> 01:31:48.570
of the level of the individual.

01:31:48.570 --> 01:31:50.900
Now detailed traces of the
level of the individual I

01:31:50.900 --> 01:31:53.780
think it's been interesting
in all three of the talks

01:31:53.780 --> 01:31:56.628
that we saw that there's this
interesting tension that it

01:31:56.628 --> 01:31:58.920
brings up both concerns as
we collect this information.

01:31:58.920 --> 01:32:03.840
But it can also be used
as a source of power

01:32:03.840 --> 01:32:05.910
for someone who potentially
is affected by these.

01:32:05.910 --> 01:32:08.470
I was certainly very
interested the relation

01:32:08.470 --> 01:32:14.010
in the third talk
about some of the ways

01:32:14.010 --> 01:32:17.310
in which these technologies
can be used on both sides.

01:32:17.310 --> 01:32:19.440
But so I think the aggregate
versus the individual,

01:32:19.440 --> 01:32:21.280
it's interesting.

01:32:21.280 --> 01:32:23.040
I also think it's a
very interesting point

01:32:23.040 --> 01:32:27.630
about the collecting more
data can be a stalling

01:32:27.630 --> 01:32:31.740
tactic, because it's interesting
I think how widespread that

01:32:31.740 --> 01:32:33.300
certainly can be in
computer science.

01:32:33.300 --> 01:32:37.128
Though if we choose areas, of
course, I don't want to say.

01:32:37.128 --> 01:32:39.420
Even if we choose quite
different areas within computer

01:32:39.420 --> 01:32:41.128
science, and I don't
want to say anything

01:32:41.128 --> 01:32:43.710
that would be controversial
for my colleagues

01:32:43.710 --> 01:32:45.240
in cryptography and security.

01:32:45.240 --> 01:32:47.550
But I think one tension
that comes up there

01:32:47.550 --> 01:32:49.920
in a very different context
is that there are systems

01:32:49.920 --> 01:32:51.480
that we know are insecure.

01:32:51.480 --> 01:32:53.400
And there comes a
point where people say,

01:32:53.400 --> 01:32:55.110
we want more attacks
on these systems

01:32:55.110 --> 01:32:56.478
to demonstrate their insecurity.

01:32:56.478 --> 01:32:58.020
What we need is a
more principled way

01:32:58.020 --> 01:32:59.320
of building them.

01:32:59.320 --> 01:33:01.650
And so I think often what
we have is the collection,

01:33:01.650 --> 01:33:03.710
the enumeration of
problems at some point

01:33:03.710 --> 01:33:05.820
need to give way to the
articulation of more

01:33:05.820 --> 01:33:08.745
principal solutions.

01:33:08.745 --> 01:33:09.370
Thanks so much.

01:33:09.370 --> 01:33:14.230
And what you meant,
so Jon mentioned

01:33:14.230 --> 01:33:18.970
in his talk about the role of
policy in terms of solutions,

01:33:18.970 --> 01:33:23.260
and then Ruha just mentioned
the kind of issue of now

01:33:23.260 --> 01:33:26.110
that there are some of these
kind of questions around bias

01:33:26.110 --> 01:33:29.140
when which there is an
immense amount of wisdom,

01:33:29.140 --> 01:33:31.510
and strategizing,
and history already.

01:33:31.510 --> 01:33:35.390
And so the next
question is for Behnaz,

01:33:35.390 --> 01:33:38.260
which is, what do you
see as the role of art

01:33:38.260 --> 01:33:43.480
and design then for maybe
influencing policy makers

01:33:43.480 --> 01:33:48.220
or for instantiating some
of the kind of practices

01:33:48.220 --> 01:33:52.430
that we already know
well from other domains?

01:33:52.430 --> 01:33:56.260
How can art and
design play a role

01:33:56.260 --> 01:33:59.340
in these kind of processes?

01:33:59.340 --> 01:34:05.940
Yeah, I think it's a great
question and in terms of I

01:34:05.940 --> 01:34:09.420
think developments of some
sort of ethical guidelines

01:34:09.420 --> 01:34:12.300
would be obviously
one way to start.

01:34:12.300 --> 01:34:15.360
But also, I really think
especially for art and design,

01:34:15.360 --> 01:34:19.770
I think one of the important
aspects of that is education.

01:34:19.770 --> 01:34:22.620
What I'm extremely
excited about is

01:34:22.620 --> 01:34:25.740
to teach next
generation of designers

01:34:25.740 --> 01:34:30.180
to be able to not look at the
systems as sort of black box

01:34:30.180 --> 01:34:32.910
that they have no clue how
it works, but they actually

01:34:32.910 --> 01:34:36.170
understand how it works, how
they can train their own data

01:34:36.170 --> 01:34:40.170
sets, how they can understand
AI system as something that they

01:34:40.170 --> 01:34:45.450
can tinker, and prototype,
and repurpose them.

01:34:45.450 --> 01:34:49.140
And don't wait for the
big, large corporates

01:34:49.140 --> 01:34:52.260
to dictate what is the
future of technology.

01:34:52.260 --> 01:34:57.300
But we actually take active
role in participating,

01:34:57.300 --> 01:35:00.340
in shaping the conversation
around the technology.

01:35:00.340 --> 01:35:05.100
So that's what I think that I
really hope that in general, we

01:35:05.100 --> 01:35:09.000
see in education a
system in art and design,

01:35:09.000 --> 01:35:13.920
particularly to really
the notion of literacy.

01:35:13.920 --> 01:35:17.550
Coming from Iran and
knowing that sometimes it

01:35:17.550 --> 01:35:21.060
can be difficult to have access
to knowledge and education

01:35:21.060 --> 01:35:24.090
or limited access, I
think it's very important

01:35:24.090 --> 01:35:26.880
to open this borders,
and open the knowledge,

01:35:26.880 --> 01:35:29.280
and share knowledge
as much as possible.

01:35:29.280 --> 01:35:33.180
If people know how AI
system, how computer vision

01:35:33.180 --> 01:35:35.820
technology works, and
they start tinkering

01:35:35.820 --> 01:35:38.580
with these technologies,
that would empower them

01:35:38.580 --> 01:35:42.120
and would empower, and open up
new possibilities for the world

01:35:42.120 --> 01:35:43.350
of art and design.

01:35:43.350 --> 01:35:47.620
So I'm extremely excited
about that possibility.

01:35:47.620 --> 01:35:48.263
Thanks so much.

01:35:48.263 --> 01:35:49.930
And one of the things
that you mentioned

01:35:49.930 --> 01:35:55.180
in your talk and
the Q&amp;A afterwards

01:35:55.180 --> 01:35:57.440
was the role of the imagination.

01:35:57.440 --> 01:36:00.730
And so what you bring to
mind our work such as Octavia

01:36:00.730 --> 01:36:04.120
Butler's, Kindred, Joanna
Russ', The Female Man,

01:36:04.120 --> 01:36:06.850
or Ralph Ellison's,
Invisible Man.

01:36:06.850 --> 01:36:09.430
So works that you
could say from one lens

01:36:09.430 --> 01:36:11.452
are all within speculative--

01:36:11.452 --> 01:36:13.660
the domain of speculative
fiction that actually think

01:36:13.660 --> 01:36:17.050
are some of the great
sociological treatises

01:36:17.050 --> 01:36:19.480
of our time in the last century.

01:36:19.480 --> 01:36:23.200
Precisely because they
can evoke and change minds

01:36:23.200 --> 01:36:26.620
through metaphor and other
types of interpretive means,

01:36:26.620 --> 01:36:33.970
not only through quantitative
or qualitative studies even.

01:36:33.970 --> 01:36:39.460
So the next question will be one
that comes from the audience.

01:36:39.460 --> 01:36:42.820
And I think this
one will go to Jon.

01:36:42.820 --> 01:36:47.500
It's is it possible to find
or create truly unbiased data?

01:36:47.500 --> 01:36:51.220
And if not, how do we
avoid imbuing any data

01:36:51.220 --> 01:36:52.555
with our own implicit biases?

01:36:55.540 --> 01:36:57.580
Yeah, obviously
a great question.

01:36:57.580 --> 01:37:01.510
And I don't think one that
there's a simple answer to.

01:37:01.510 --> 01:37:03.970
But I mean, I think
if you were going

01:37:03.970 --> 01:37:06.670
to make the argument
that there really

01:37:06.670 --> 01:37:09.037
is no such thing
as unbiased data,

01:37:09.037 --> 01:37:10.870
I think you would start
from the observation

01:37:10.870 --> 01:37:13.840
that it's in the
nature of computation

01:37:13.840 --> 01:37:15.790
to work inside an abstraction.

01:37:15.790 --> 01:37:21.230
I mean, I think that's both
the power in the limitation

01:37:21.230 --> 01:37:23.200
of the computational approach.

01:37:23.200 --> 01:37:24.760
As computer
scientists, were taught

01:37:24.760 --> 01:37:29.260
to create abstraction barriers
between the world, which

01:37:29.260 --> 01:37:32.530
is very complicated in the
system that we're building,

01:37:32.530 --> 01:37:35.450
the system that
we're working in.

01:37:35.450 --> 01:37:39.593
And so I think, and that
again gives computer science--

01:37:39.593 --> 01:37:41.260
I think abstraction
barriers [INAUDIBLE]

01:37:41.260 --> 01:37:43.035
technological systems
much of their power.

01:37:43.035 --> 01:37:44.660
There are many examples
of this, right?

01:37:44.660 --> 01:37:47.710
The fact that you can in
non pandemic times perhaps

01:37:47.710 --> 01:37:50.890
fly into an airport,
rent a car, and start

01:37:50.890 --> 01:37:53.440
driving that car
down the highway,

01:37:53.440 --> 01:37:55.870
a dangerous, potentially
life threatening activity.

01:37:55.870 --> 01:37:57.370
It's a car you've
never seen before.

01:37:57.370 --> 01:37:58.600
You have no idea how
the engine works,

01:37:58.600 --> 01:38:00.730
but because it presents to you
the abstraction of a steering

01:38:00.730 --> 01:38:02.620
wheel, a gas pedal,
and a brake pedal,

01:38:02.620 --> 01:38:05.313
you're able to put
your life in its hands

01:38:05.313 --> 01:38:06.980
even though you've
never seen it before.

01:38:06.980 --> 01:38:08.938
So abstraction is an
incredibly powerful thing.

01:38:08.938 --> 01:38:11.320
But abstraction is
extremely reductive, right?

01:38:11.320 --> 01:38:12.730
The car might be a hybrid car.

01:38:12.730 --> 01:38:14.920
It might be some new
high-tech kind of engine.

01:38:14.920 --> 01:38:16.900
We don't really
know how it works.

01:38:16.900 --> 01:38:20.220
It's a similar way I
think any act of building

01:38:20.220 --> 01:38:22.630
a computational model is
that same kind of reduction.

01:38:22.630 --> 01:38:24.970
And so there's going to
be design decisions that

01:38:24.970 --> 01:38:25.668
go into that.

01:38:25.668 --> 01:38:27.460
I think that's the
sense in which data will

01:38:27.460 --> 01:38:30.040
be inevitably imbued with bias.

01:38:30.040 --> 01:38:33.130
I think now this is
the part of the answer

01:38:33.130 --> 01:38:37.857
that I think is in some ways
more aspirational than concrete

01:38:37.857 --> 01:38:38.440
at the moment.

01:38:38.440 --> 01:38:41.080
But I think one of
the things I think

01:38:41.080 --> 01:38:44.620
is promising in the kinds of, in
the way research has been going

01:38:44.620 --> 01:38:48.730
over the past several years
is the really rapidly widening

01:38:48.730 --> 01:38:52.030
scope of the conversation around
the construction of data sets,

01:38:52.030 --> 01:38:54.040
the construction of
computational models

01:38:54.040 --> 01:38:56.140
to understand both
the computer science

01:38:56.140 --> 01:38:57.940
side and outside the
computer science.

01:38:57.940 --> 01:39:02.280
That this is a
first order activity

01:39:02.280 --> 01:39:04.400
that everyone needs
to participate in.

01:39:04.400 --> 01:39:07.640
That this is not
inside any one field.

01:39:07.640 --> 01:39:10.480
And I think that
I think is going

01:39:10.480 --> 01:39:14.200
to be a way to at least
begin making progress.

01:39:16.910 --> 01:39:17.690
Thanks so much.

01:39:17.690 --> 01:39:21.728
So if I can just insert
a couple of my own views,

01:39:21.728 --> 01:39:23.270
and then I'll have
the next question,

01:39:23.270 --> 01:39:26.030
which is for Ruha Benjamin.

01:39:26.030 --> 01:39:28.600
So I just wanted
to say something,

01:39:28.600 --> 01:39:30.460
because my book,
Phantasmal Media,

01:39:30.460 --> 01:39:32.290
An Approach to Imagination,
Computation, and Expression,

01:39:32.290 --> 01:39:33.040
was on the screen.

01:39:33.040 --> 01:39:34.630
I saw it before I spoke.

01:39:34.630 --> 01:39:38.740
The central idea is that while
people often think of computing

01:39:38.740 --> 01:39:45.310
as objective, utilitarian, and
acultural, actually computing

01:39:45.310 --> 01:39:47.800
implements various
forms of subjectivity

01:39:47.800 --> 01:39:52.030
is always cultural, and can
be used to spark criticality.

01:39:52.030 --> 01:39:55.810
And so I didn't write--

01:39:55.810 --> 01:39:58.570
this was from a while ago
before the term algorithmic bias

01:39:58.570 --> 01:40:00.700
was quite in parlance.

01:40:00.700 --> 01:40:04.060
But one of the key ideas was
that all technical systems

01:40:04.060 --> 01:40:05.650
are cultural systems.

01:40:05.650 --> 01:40:07.390
And just as an
example, even something

01:40:07.390 --> 01:40:09.057
like the stored program
of architecture,

01:40:09.057 --> 01:40:11.350
the [INAUDIBLE]
machine, it's in all,

01:40:11.350 --> 01:40:13.450
nearly every computing
system we use

01:40:13.450 --> 01:40:15.228
is grounded in the
culture of its time.

01:40:15.228 --> 01:40:17.020
If you go back and read
the original paper,

01:40:17.020 --> 01:40:19.870
it's not abstracted like
the way we describe now.

01:40:19.870 --> 01:40:22.935
It describes inputs and
outputs, "Organs of machines"

01:40:22.935 --> 01:40:25.060
and it says computers should
be made of vacuum tube

01:40:25.060 --> 01:40:26.685
elements, and all of
these other things

01:40:26.685 --> 01:40:28.540
that we have left
by the wayside that

01:40:28.540 --> 01:40:32.020
are entirely rounded
within its time period

01:40:32.020 --> 01:40:33.153
and cultural history.

01:40:33.153 --> 01:40:34.570
And so I think
it's just something

01:40:34.570 --> 01:40:37.180
we need to be aware of is
that even systems we think

01:40:37.180 --> 01:40:39.730
of as most objective
and acultural

01:40:39.730 --> 01:40:42.770
implement various
forms of culture.

01:40:42.770 --> 01:40:47.400
And so the question
that I have for Ruha,

01:40:47.400 --> 01:40:52.090
I think it was sparked by
something that Benhaz said,

01:40:52.090 --> 01:40:57.340
but I think that it resonates
with your talk as well, which

01:40:57.340 --> 01:40:59.270
is about tools of resistance.

01:40:59.270 --> 01:41:03.250
So Benhaz spoke about
artistic tools of resistance.

01:41:03.250 --> 01:41:04.960
The question is broader.

01:41:04.960 --> 01:41:06.730
And I think it's
relevant for you

01:41:06.730 --> 01:41:08.950
because you talk
about communities

01:41:08.950 --> 01:41:13.090
and how communities can
resist against certain types

01:41:13.090 --> 01:41:16.840
of technologies
that impose bias.

01:41:16.840 --> 01:41:19.630
And so the question is, are
tools of resistance ones that

01:41:19.630 --> 01:41:23.260
cannot be co-opted by
those being resisted?

01:41:23.260 --> 01:41:26.920
And if I can add, are tools
that cannot be co-opted even

01:41:26.920 --> 01:41:27.590
possible?

01:41:27.590 --> 01:41:31.640
So anyway, that's my question.

01:41:31.640 --> 01:41:32.890
I appreciate that.

01:41:32.890 --> 01:41:35.500
And just before
I jump into this,

01:41:35.500 --> 01:41:37.990
I also wanted to build
on something else

01:41:37.990 --> 01:41:40.930
Benhaz was saying
about the role of art,

01:41:40.930 --> 01:41:44.950
the arts in terms of
our ethical framework.

01:41:44.950 --> 01:41:49.690
And I would say in terms of our
imagination, one of the things

01:41:49.690 --> 01:41:55.630
that I really want to insist
upon is that the arts shouldn't

01:41:55.630 --> 01:41:57.760
be an afterthought, it
shouldn't be something

01:41:57.760 --> 01:42:01.930
that we bring in after the
algorithms are designed

01:42:01.930 --> 01:42:04.270
or after the technical
system in order

01:42:04.270 --> 01:42:06.640
to create a sheen or a gloss.

01:42:06.640 --> 01:42:12.310
But really to understand that
imagination is a battlefield.

01:42:12.310 --> 01:42:15.400
And that in terms
of imagination,

01:42:15.400 --> 01:42:18.190
certain imaginations
are being codified

01:42:18.190 --> 01:42:19.930
in our technical systems.

01:42:19.930 --> 01:42:23.230
And one of the ways to
understand the role of the arts

01:42:23.230 --> 01:42:25.840
is to radically
expand our imagination

01:42:25.840 --> 01:42:27.520
through the questions
that are asked,

01:42:27.520 --> 01:42:29.562
through the different
frameworks that are brought

01:42:29.562 --> 01:42:31.460
to bear ethical and otherwise.

01:42:31.460 --> 01:42:36.880
And so again, to understand
the centrality of imagination

01:42:36.880 --> 01:42:39.880
and everything that we're
doing, not simply in the realm

01:42:39.880 --> 01:42:41.440
that are dubbed the arts.

01:42:41.440 --> 01:42:43.240
And so that's
something I just want

01:42:43.240 --> 01:42:46.510
to put on the table, which
relates to this idea of tools.

01:42:46.510 --> 01:42:49.690
I don't think there
are any tools that

01:42:49.690 --> 01:42:52.570
are immune from co-optation.

01:42:52.570 --> 01:42:55.810
But what I would
suggest is that in terms

01:42:55.810 --> 01:42:59.650
of trying to prevent that
understanding, that tool

01:42:59.650 --> 01:43:06.260
making, one it doesn't just
apply to hardware and software.

01:43:06.260 --> 01:43:10.450
So one of the things that I
really want us to consider

01:43:10.450 --> 01:43:15.670
are the many types of social
tools, tools of interaction

01:43:15.670 --> 01:43:19.330
relationality that don't
usually come to mind when

01:43:19.330 --> 01:43:20.950
we say the word technology.

01:43:20.950 --> 01:43:23.410
And so to have a much more
expansive understanding of what

01:43:23.410 --> 01:43:26.980
counts as tools, and
to understand that

01:43:26.980 --> 01:43:31.750
in terms of the power, the way
that power is laden in tools,

01:43:31.750 --> 01:43:35.470
it's not a one-time
thing where you

01:43:35.470 --> 01:43:37.900
at the point of
creation of something

01:43:37.900 --> 01:43:40.660
that it sets it off in
a certain trajectory.

01:43:40.660 --> 01:43:42.520
Either we're helping
the oppressed

01:43:42.520 --> 01:43:44.920
or empowering certain groups.

01:43:44.920 --> 01:43:47.440
But it's an ongoing battle.

01:43:47.440 --> 01:43:49.780
It's an ongoing
form of resistance.

01:43:49.780 --> 01:43:51.670
And so in that
case, I don't think

01:43:51.670 --> 01:43:57.280
that there's a way to immunize
any of our technologies

01:43:57.280 --> 01:43:59.440
from co-optation
or from being used

01:43:59.440 --> 01:44:01.940
in ways that were not intended.

01:44:01.940 --> 01:44:05.650
And so that's why we have
to build into the process,

01:44:05.650 --> 01:44:10.030
form some accountability
where we continually calibrate

01:44:10.030 --> 01:44:13.180
and think about how
and if these tools

01:44:13.180 --> 01:44:19.050
are being used to reinforce the
status quo, reinforce power,

01:44:19.050 --> 01:44:22.950
and inequality or being used to
subvert it and to transform it.

01:44:22.950 --> 01:44:26.310
And so it really has to
be an ongoing process

01:44:26.310 --> 01:44:29.580
of accountability that's
institutionalized that's not

01:44:29.580 --> 01:44:33.630
simply left to individuals
who may or may not

01:44:33.630 --> 01:44:34.740
have good intentions.

01:44:34.740 --> 01:44:36.900
I think the stakes are too high.

01:44:36.900 --> 01:44:39.390
To think of the
issues that we're

01:44:39.390 --> 01:44:41.310
wrestling with on
this panel simply

01:44:41.310 --> 01:44:44.430
in terms of ethics, or the
ethics of individuals, or even

01:44:44.430 --> 01:44:45.270
fields.

01:44:45.270 --> 01:44:47.310
But we really have
to build and creative

01:44:47.310 --> 01:44:50.430
ecosystem, forms of
regulation, accountability,

01:44:50.430 --> 01:44:55.080
protections that operate
whether or not individuals

01:44:55.080 --> 01:44:56.460
have the best intentions.

01:44:56.460 --> 01:45:02.290
I think we can't rest our, build
our futures on the well-meaning

01:45:02.290 --> 01:45:07.930
intent not to do harm,
as important as that is.

01:45:07.930 --> 01:45:10.930
So what I love in
your response are,

01:45:10.930 --> 01:45:12.430
well, I was going
to say two things.

01:45:12.430 --> 01:45:14.260
But actually all of
it, but two things

01:45:14.260 --> 01:45:16.360
I'll call attention
to in particular

01:45:16.360 --> 01:45:21.680
were one, this idea about
computing as a tool.

01:45:21.680 --> 01:45:24.220
It's a very instrumental
view of computing,

01:45:24.220 --> 01:45:28.190
because computing can be
used in so many broader ways.

01:45:28.190 --> 01:45:31.780
Whether it's purely
for the austere

01:45:31.780 --> 01:45:34.420
beauty of formalization.

01:45:34.420 --> 01:45:39.260
Or it could be something such
as in some of the communities

01:45:39.260 --> 01:45:42.970
that I work in, seeing the
computer as a medium, not just

01:45:42.970 --> 01:45:43.610
as a tool.

01:45:43.610 --> 01:45:46.360
So when you ask that question,
can media be co-opted?

01:45:46.360 --> 01:45:49.660
Then it becomes a little bit
more obvious than when you say,

01:45:49.660 --> 01:45:51.333
can a tool be co-opted?

01:45:51.333 --> 01:45:52.750
And then finally,
well, the reason

01:45:52.750 --> 01:45:54.958
I wanted to bring this
together with the second point

01:45:54.958 --> 01:45:58.360
that you made was about the
arts not being an afterthought.

01:45:58.360 --> 01:46:00.190
Because I think
oftentimes that comes

01:46:00.190 --> 01:46:03.228
from when the arts are seen
in an instrumental way.

01:46:03.228 --> 01:46:05.770
And so let's say if someone's
applying for a National Science

01:46:05.770 --> 01:46:07.885
Foundation grant,
and then say, OK, I

01:46:07.885 --> 01:46:09.010
have my intellectual merit.

01:46:09.010 --> 01:46:12.100
Arts will be broader impact,
I'll bring in an artist

01:46:12.100 --> 01:46:15.790
afterwards and they can help
me to submit dissemination.

01:46:15.790 --> 01:46:19.420
When arts are thought of
in that instrumental way,

01:46:19.420 --> 01:46:23.710
it often leads to them being
that kind of afterthought.

01:46:23.710 --> 01:46:26.830
And so the next question
is for Behnaz, which is,

01:46:26.830 --> 01:46:30.910
I think related to this
idea of an instrumental view

01:46:30.910 --> 01:46:35.700
of technologies being co-opted.

01:46:35.700 --> 01:46:39.910
And so the question is
asking about your thoughts

01:46:39.910 --> 01:46:42.670
around gender that
stem from your work,

01:46:42.670 --> 01:46:50.350
and the way that
your work operates

01:46:50.350 --> 01:46:53.140
as a critique of
heteronormativity

01:46:53.140 --> 01:46:55.570
of heterosexual normativity.

01:46:55.570 --> 01:46:59.590
But they're also
wondering about the ways

01:46:59.590 --> 01:47:04.240
that heterosexual normatively
might be reinforced

01:47:04.240 --> 01:47:05.570
by this kind of work.

01:47:05.570 --> 01:47:13.150
And then they ask know how
a gender fluid perspective

01:47:13.150 --> 01:47:16.480
on the issue of
gaze or prospective

01:47:16.480 --> 01:47:20.950
influenced by queer theory might
inform your work differently.

01:47:20.950 --> 01:47:23.710
And then just as the
final editorial remark

01:47:23.710 --> 01:47:27.580
on their question, because we
talked about deep learning.

01:47:27.580 --> 01:47:30.160
With deep state
technologies, the major way

01:47:30.160 --> 01:47:33.340
that they're being used,
the most prevalent use

01:47:33.340 --> 01:47:35.478
is actually sexual
objectification,

01:47:35.478 --> 01:47:36.270
like you mentioned.

01:47:36.270 --> 01:47:40.770
In fact, creating nude pictures
of celebrities and so forth.

01:47:40.770 --> 01:47:44.140
That's the most prevalent use
of those technologies out there,

01:47:44.140 --> 01:47:48.100
which seems very related to
this idea of a sexualizing gaze.

01:47:48.100 --> 01:47:51.580
So what would the lenses of
gender fluidity and queer

01:47:51.580 --> 01:47:55.230
theory bring to the kind
of work that you do?

01:47:55.230 --> 01:47:57.450
Well that's a difficult one.

01:47:57.450 --> 01:48:02.430
But I'll try to answer
as best as I can.

01:48:02.430 --> 01:48:04.890
So it's fascinating
because, for instance,

01:48:04.890 --> 01:48:07.350
in [INAUDIBLE] of the
gaze, the project that

01:48:07.350 --> 01:48:12.270
attract the gaze
of the onlooker,

01:48:12.270 --> 01:48:15.240
I was using a system
that it was pre-trained.

01:48:15.240 --> 01:48:20.700
I was using a pre-trained model
that it was basically able to

01:48:20.700 --> 01:48:24.330
or predicting the gender
in a very binary way.

01:48:24.330 --> 01:48:26.910
Which we know is
very problematic

01:48:26.910 --> 01:48:29.350
if there is a male or a female.

01:48:29.350 --> 01:48:33.600
So as a designer, although
I use both systems

01:48:33.600 --> 01:48:37.560
that they are trained
based on pre-trained data

01:48:37.560 --> 01:48:42.120
or in some cases, I train the
system with a certain data.

01:48:42.120 --> 01:48:45.930
I think it's fascinating
to just address

01:48:45.930 --> 01:48:49.170
these issues and particularly
as you mentioned regarding

01:48:49.170 --> 01:48:51.390
let's say, gender fluidity.

01:48:51.390 --> 01:48:53.820
Can computational
system really predict

01:48:53.820 --> 01:48:57.610
through the pure representation
or identification?

01:48:57.610 --> 01:48:59.680
And I think folks from
that point of view,

01:48:59.680 --> 01:49:03.000
your work particularly
actually address these issues

01:49:03.000 --> 01:49:07.260
that how our identity
is being represented

01:49:07.260 --> 01:49:10.840
in virtual environments.

01:49:10.840 --> 01:49:14.460
And I think a lot
of times, for me,

01:49:14.460 --> 01:49:20.280
is to either use them
or question them.

01:49:20.280 --> 01:49:24.060
I think also I have to
mention that this notion

01:49:24.060 --> 01:49:29.760
of essentialism, which is very
dominant in feminist discourse.

01:49:29.760 --> 01:49:31.530
Could we actually
say that there is

01:49:31.530 --> 01:49:35.700
anything essential
about being a female

01:49:35.700 --> 01:49:38.220
and having the
female experience?

01:49:38.220 --> 01:49:43.560
Obviously, it's been in
Western way of thinking.

01:49:43.560 --> 01:49:47.070
And there are many
theories that question

01:49:47.070 --> 01:49:50.520
that sort of essentialism
in art and design practices.

01:49:50.520 --> 01:49:53.730
Having said that, also I have
to acknowledge that coming

01:49:53.730 --> 01:49:58.740
from a culture that it's
mainly male dominance,

01:49:58.740 --> 01:50:02.850
you are still struggling with
a lot of this gender binary

01:50:02.850 --> 01:50:05.110
differences.

01:50:05.110 --> 01:50:13.380
So I don't have a answer how
computational systems could

01:50:13.380 --> 01:50:17.660
address the gender fluidity,
because it's absolutely

01:50:17.660 --> 01:50:21.750
a complicated topic, but I
think it's also very, very

01:50:21.750 --> 01:50:24.630
interesting to think
about how are we going

01:50:24.630 --> 01:50:26.740
to represent our identities?

01:50:26.740 --> 01:50:28.920
So I would like to also
hear from you folks.

01:50:28.920 --> 01:50:32.700
What are your thoughts on that?

01:50:32.700 --> 01:50:34.910
Sure, and so speak
briefly and then

01:50:34.910 --> 01:50:36.940
I have another question for Jon.

01:50:36.940 --> 01:50:41.508
And so one, is trying to
reconcile between domains by,

01:50:41.508 --> 01:50:43.550
so I mentioned the computer
science [INAUDIBLE],,

01:50:43.550 --> 01:50:46.840
I also have degrees
in art as well.

01:50:46.840 --> 01:50:48.590
And so one of the
things I have to do then

01:50:48.590 --> 01:50:51.530
is think about the values
within each domain.

01:50:51.530 --> 01:50:53.930
So is it something
like generalizability?

01:50:53.930 --> 01:50:57.350
Is it a value like how
exhaustive and efficient

01:50:57.350 --> 01:50:59.450
is this particular
algorithm that I've created?

01:50:59.450 --> 01:51:01.730
Or is it something like
can the system spark

01:51:01.730 --> 01:51:05.000
criticality and within society?

01:51:05.000 --> 01:51:06.890
Or can it even be
used practical?

01:51:06.890 --> 01:51:08.883
And can you-- this
is maybe more like

01:51:08.883 --> 01:51:10.550
some of the work in
the social sciences.

01:51:10.550 --> 01:51:15.340
Can you demonstrate that
this work empirically sparks

01:51:15.340 --> 01:51:18.170
reflection, say using some
kind of validated instrument?

01:51:18.170 --> 01:51:20.930
And so when reconciling
between those

01:51:20.930 --> 01:51:24.170
domains, then I
often find insights

01:51:24.170 --> 01:51:26.720
such as when
critiquing a system,

01:51:26.720 --> 01:51:28.400
let's say a computer
science system that

01:51:28.400 --> 01:51:33.110
was created to help
girls learn mathematics,

01:51:33.110 --> 01:51:37.910
then they can ask questions such
as, but what model of gender

01:51:37.910 --> 01:51:39.560
is this system working under?

01:51:39.560 --> 01:51:41.750
Is this a biological
positivist model

01:51:41.750 --> 01:51:44.180
of gender in which all
girls are assumed to learn

01:51:44.180 --> 01:51:45.590
in the same kind of way?

01:51:45.590 --> 01:51:49.160
Or is it something where their
girlhood is not necessarily

01:51:49.160 --> 01:51:52.520
understood to be something
which is monolithic across them?

01:51:52.520 --> 01:51:55.190
So then you can interrogate
the technical practices

01:51:55.190 --> 01:51:56.990
in a very different
way when you begin

01:51:56.990 --> 01:51:59.750
to think about how
it's instantiating

01:51:59.750 --> 01:52:00.860
particular sets of values.

01:52:00.860 --> 01:52:04.070
And so I think what you were
mentioning in particular,

01:52:04.070 --> 01:52:07.550
I have one patented
computational system,

01:52:07.550 --> 01:52:09.260
which is for
representing identity

01:52:09.260 --> 01:52:11.330
very differently than
in the top-down way

01:52:11.330 --> 01:52:13.200
many computational systems do.

01:52:13.200 --> 01:52:16.160
It's essentially not trying
to put you into a box

01:52:16.160 --> 01:52:18.080
by some set number of fields.

01:52:18.080 --> 01:52:19.700
But actually
implements fluidity,

01:52:19.700 --> 01:52:22.220
that is you have a trajectory
in which you naturalize

01:52:22.220 --> 01:52:24.200
into categories, the
[INAUDIBLE] of membership

01:52:24.200 --> 01:52:26.230
in categories,
multiple categories.

01:52:26.230 --> 01:52:29.520
So your identity isn't fixed
at any one particular location.

01:52:29.520 --> 01:52:31.970
And it can be applied to so
many different things, ranging

01:52:31.970 --> 01:52:34.790
from music, because
our tastes might

01:52:34.790 --> 01:52:36.890
be different in the morning
than in the evening.

01:52:36.890 --> 01:52:41.060
Or some of the kind
of issues of bias

01:52:41.060 --> 01:52:43.410
that we've spoken about
in conversation here.

01:52:43.410 --> 01:52:45.850
I'll leave it at that.

01:52:45.850 --> 01:52:47.900
And maybe could continue later.

01:52:47.900 --> 01:52:49.450
But I would want to just--

01:52:49.450 --> 01:52:51.570
Maybe I can just one
more thing, folks?

01:52:51.570 --> 01:52:52.070
Sure.

01:52:52.070 --> 01:52:55.250
That this somehow we
are complicit in this.

01:52:55.250 --> 01:52:58.760
I mean, the fact that
we already have LGBTQ.

01:52:58.760 --> 01:53:02.780
We have this form of
identification that at the end,

01:53:02.780 --> 01:53:05.030
people have to put
themselves in the boxes

01:53:05.030 --> 01:53:07.550
to identify as one
of those categories.

01:53:07.550 --> 01:53:10.460
This would sort of originates
from that point of view,

01:53:10.460 --> 01:53:14.450
and then replicates in
our computational systems.

01:53:14.450 --> 01:53:17.030
So my point is
that it's the issue

01:53:17.030 --> 01:53:21.740
is so complex because in our
actual way that we are also

01:53:21.740 --> 01:53:25.430
identifying the society,
we are constantly creating

01:53:25.430 --> 01:53:27.920
these boxes that you mentioned.

01:53:27.920 --> 01:53:29.780
And we add more categories.

01:53:29.780 --> 01:53:32.720
And we think that by
adding more categories,

01:53:32.720 --> 01:53:35.600
everyone should find
their own identification.

01:53:35.600 --> 01:53:42.770
But we truly have to
question how we operate, even

01:53:42.770 --> 01:53:44.270
in the society in
the first place,

01:53:44.270 --> 01:53:46.840
rather than how the
system also replicate

01:53:46.840 --> 01:53:49.790
in computational systems.

01:53:49.790 --> 01:53:51.410
Right, that's exactly right.

01:53:51.410 --> 01:53:53.090
I mean, I use that
term box effects

01:53:53.090 --> 01:53:56.090
to describe all the kind
of discriminatory fallout

01:53:56.090 --> 01:53:58.400
that comes from that kind
of taxonomic perspective

01:53:58.400 --> 01:53:59.930
on identity.

01:53:59.930 --> 01:54:02.750
And maybe more evocatively,
Jeff [? Valcore ?] and Lee

01:54:02.750 --> 01:54:05.210
[? Star ?] the term
identity torque,

01:54:05.210 --> 01:54:08.120
this idea that when you
don't fit into those identity

01:54:08.120 --> 01:54:10.080
categories being
forced by society,

01:54:10.080 --> 01:54:12.680
it actually produces
torque on the individual.

01:54:12.680 --> 01:54:18.080
That is , actual emotional pain
because people have experiences

01:54:18.080 --> 01:54:20.980
that they can't reconcile
with how they see themselves.

01:54:20.980 --> 01:54:25.250
And so there's so much
more we can say about this.

01:54:25.250 --> 01:54:27.920
But such important points.

01:54:27.920 --> 01:54:31.700
And so one question that
we have from the audience,

01:54:31.700 --> 01:54:33.500
actually from Will
Lockett who was

01:54:33.500 --> 01:54:38.810
one of the coorganizers of
the Unfolding Intelligence

01:54:38.810 --> 01:54:43.460
Symposium asked about the
capacity of mathematics

01:54:43.460 --> 01:54:45.690
to represent.

01:54:45.690 --> 01:54:47.810
So I'll use his terms,
and then also say the way

01:54:47.810 --> 01:54:50.150
that I interpret it,
which is the capricious

01:54:50.150 --> 01:54:52.490
specificity of
individual behavior

01:54:52.490 --> 01:54:55.430
as in some agent
based simulation.

01:54:55.430 --> 01:54:58.760
And I think really it's very
similar to the first question

01:54:58.760 --> 01:55:00.885
that I asked.

01:55:00.885 --> 01:55:02.510
Actually the ending
question in the Q&amp;A

01:55:02.510 --> 01:55:06.710
after Jon's session,
which was, is computing,

01:55:06.710 --> 01:55:11.270
is using mathematical approaches
always going to be reductive?

01:55:11.270 --> 01:55:13.490
Or is there some way
that those approaches

01:55:13.490 --> 01:55:18.020
can be used that are
complementary to other types

01:55:18.020 --> 01:55:20.750
of approaches, and are not
necessarily going to be

01:55:20.750 --> 01:55:22.610
reductive in harmful ways?

01:55:25.590 --> 01:55:27.360
Yeah, it's a great question.

01:55:27.360 --> 01:55:30.150
And I mean, I think it's
a question that comes up

01:55:30.150 --> 01:55:33.210
a lot when we talk about
building mathematical models

01:55:33.210 --> 01:55:35.310
of the world.

01:55:35.310 --> 01:55:37.470
And I think in particular,
mathematical models

01:55:37.470 --> 01:55:40.490
of the social world.

01:55:40.490 --> 01:55:43.490
And know it already
begins with the fact

01:55:43.490 --> 01:55:46.742
that a lot of people's
education trains them

01:55:46.742 --> 01:55:48.950
in the intuitions around
building mathematical models

01:55:48.950 --> 01:55:51.350
of simple physical systems.

01:55:51.350 --> 01:55:53.290
And there's always
this optimistic sense

01:55:53.290 --> 01:55:55.040
that maybe I can take
what I learned there

01:55:55.040 --> 01:55:59.840
and begin to apply it to the
situations in the social world.

01:55:59.840 --> 01:56:03.770
And obviously, we've run into
an enormous number of challenges

01:56:03.770 --> 01:56:04.340
there.

01:56:04.340 --> 01:56:06.570
In particular, that
the state of the world,

01:56:06.570 --> 01:56:10.850
in the case of the social world,
typically has an extremely long

01:56:10.850 --> 01:56:11.730
description, right?

01:56:11.730 --> 01:56:14.858
In the language of features,
it's extremely wide.

01:56:14.858 --> 01:56:16.400
There are many, many
different things

01:56:16.400 --> 01:56:21.795
that I know about an individual,
what their experiences are,

01:56:21.795 --> 01:56:24.170
what their actions are, what
their motivations and intent

01:56:24.170 --> 01:56:24.740
are.

01:56:24.740 --> 01:56:29.210
And as a result
to represent that,

01:56:29.210 --> 01:56:31.070
it's something where
we can't really

01:56:31.070 --> 01:56:36.170
hope to fully either know or
represent all those things.

01:56:36.170 --> 01:56:41.390
And that's why I think we end up
with some of these challenges.

01:56:41.390 --> 01:56:45.758
So I think I personally
tend toward optimism

01:56:45.758 --> 01:56:48.050
that this is an activity that
we should be engaging in,

01:56:48.050 --> 01:56:50.960
because I think it's
a valuable supplement

01:56:50.960 --> 01:56:54.290
to the kinds of discussions
which are taking place.

01:56:54.290 --> 01:56:57.530
But we certainly I don't
think see any evidence

01:56:57.530 --> 01:57:02.490
that it's a substitute for
these other forms of analysis.

01:57:02.490 --> 01:57:05.480
I think it supplements it.

01:57:05.480 --> 01:57:07.790
And if we think of
it as a supplement,

01:57:07.790 --> 01:57:11.580
then we can begin to look
for ways in the spirit

01:57:11.580 --> 01:57:14.630
that I think has been
true in all the talks.

01:57:14.630 --> 01:57:17.330
I think Benhaz,
that you [INAUDIBLE]

01:57:17.330 --> 01:57:21.050
very powerfully the multiple
uses of the same tool

01:57:21.050 --> 01:57:26.630
or the same methodology to look
for ways in which reductiveness

01:57:26.630 --> 01:57:30.230
can sometimes
expose commonalities

01:57:30.230 --> 01:57:33.660
across situations that we
think of as very different.

01:57:33.660 --> 01:57:35.822
And I think if that's
done in partnership

01:57:35.822 --> 01:57:38.030
with the conversation that
recognizes the differences

01:57:38.030 --> 01:57:39.800
across these
domains, then it can

01:57:39.800 --> 01:57:42.118
establish linkages
that may actually

01:57:42.118 --> 01:57:43.160
provoke further thinking.

01:57:43.160 --> 01:57:47.450
I think if it's done to in the
absence of that conversation,

01:57:47.450 --> 01:57:55.090
then it just ends up obscuring
important distinctions.

01:57:55.090 --> 01:57:57.100
Thanks, it reminds
me of a statement

01:57:57.100 --> 01:58:02.110
made by the late great computer
scientist, Joseph Gogan, who

01:58:02.110 --> 01:58:06.460
said something to the
effect of, we can describe

01:58:06.460 --> 01:58:11.590
subjective social phenomena in
subjective ways, critical ways.

01:58:11.590 --> 01:58:16.270
And we can also describe them in
precise ways, and it doesn't--

01:58:16.270 --> 01:58:18.610
none of these it gives
additional authority

01:58:18.610 --> 01:58:19.407
to the analysis.

01:58:19.407 --> 01:58:21.740
They're just different languages
for doing the analysis.

01:58:21.740 --> 01:58:23.157
And so when you
say complementary,

01:58:23.157 --> 01:58:25.390
I feel like that's
one of the keys,

01:58:25.390 --> 01:58:30.070
just understanding whether it's
the imaginative like Behnaz's

01:58:30.070 --> 01:58:34.460
and Ruha's work as well,
because I've seen her work

01:58:34.460 --> 01:58:37.150
bridge with the arts, too.

01:58:37.150 --> 01:58:42.100
Or the mathematical, it's
just different lenses

01:58:42.100 --> 01:58:44.770
that, again, can be
complementary in addressing

01:58:44.770 --> 01:58:47.690
related issues.

01:58:47.690 --> 01:58:51.040
And so I want to
also just, since we

01:58:51.040 --> 01:58:53.200
have just a few
more minutes left,

01:58:53.200 --> 01:58:56.940
ask if any of the panelists
have questions for one another.

01:59:02.240 --> 01:59:06.720
I'll take the opportunity, not
necessarily to pose a question,

01:59:06.720 --> 01:59:08.450
but just to be in conversation.

01:59:08.450 --> 01:59:10.440
And two quick things.

01:59:10.440 --> 01:59:14.990
One is on your last
point, Fox, about and also

01:59:14.990 --> 01:59:17.120
Jon's point about
the complementary,

01:59:17.120 --> 01:59:20.210
I want us to think
about that in relation

01:59:20.210 --> 01:59:25.010
to the very real hierarchies
of knowledge in the academy

01:59:25.010 --> 01:59:29.090
that while ideally these
fields would be complementary,

01:59:29.090 --> 01:59:31.250
there are certain
forms of knowledge that

01:59:31.250 --> 01:59:35.240
have more, that are granted more
authority and more legitimacy.

01:59:35.240 --> 01:59:38.660
That even in collaboration,
trump other forms of knowledge.

01:59:38.660 --> 01:59:40.920
And they follow along
predictable lines,

01:59:40.920 --> 01:59:44.450
hard to soft, the qualitative
to quantitative, et cetera.

01:59:44.450 --> 01:59:46.550
And at the same
time, I just want

01:59:46.550 --> 01:59:49.460
to say that we've
spent some time talking

01:59:49.460 --> 01:59:51.770
about the relationship
between computation

01:59:51.770 --> 01:59:55.130
and reductive racist,
sexist, forms of hierarchy.

01:59:55.130 --> 01:59:59.120
But I also want to call my
own fields into complicity

01:59:59.120 --> 02:00:01.580
the social sciences
and the humanities,

02:00:01.580 --> 02:00:05.600
and that storytelling can be
just as violent, and just as

02:00:05.600 --> 02:00:09.920
reductive in terms of
infusing all kinds of meaning

02:00:09.920 --> 02:00:10.710
in the world.

02:00:10.710 --> 02:00:13.610
So it's not simply
on the statistical

02:00:13.610 --> 02:00:16.580
or the computational side
that this kind of reckoning

02:00:16.580 --> 02:00:20.300
needs to happen, but also in the
humanities and social sciences

02:00:20.300 --> 02:00:22.970
that have been some
of the major producers

02:00:22.970 --> 02:00:26.930
of this racist architecture
that we still inhabit.

02:00:26.930 --> 02:00:29.420
And so no field is
immune from the kind

02:00:29.420 --> 02:00:35.000
of questions and reckoning that
I think we're talking about.

02:00:35.000 --> 02:00:35.930
Such important points.

02:00:35.930 --> 02:00:39.020
They both come to mind this
notion of cultural humility,

02:00:39.020 --> 02:00:42.000
which often is not used related
to academic disciplines.

02:00:42.000 --> 02:00:44.420
But I think you give an
exact, a good description

02:00:44.420 --> 02:00:48.200
of why it should be applied
to those such disciplines.

02:00:48.200 --> 02:00:56.040
And so we're coming down to
just our last seconds here.

02:00:56.040 --> 02:00:59.660
So I appreciated
that final remark

02:00:59.660 --> 02:01:01.850
in terms of bringing
the different ideas

02:01:01.850 --> 02:01:04.860
into conversation
with one another.

02:01:04.860 --> 02:01:09.800
I would like just to welcome
if Jon and then Behnaz have

02:01:09.800 --> 02:01:12.240
just very brief
closing remarks too,

02:01:12.240 --> 02:01:18.480
then we can do so and
then end the panel.

02:01:18.480 --> 02:01:22.930
I first of all, I would
say it's been a privilege

02:01:22.930 --> 02:01:27.160
to be able to take part in the
conversation with all of you

02:01:27.160 --> 02:01:29.550
and with the insights
that have been expressed

02:01:29.550 --> 02:01:30.550
across different fields.

02:01:30.550 --> 02:01:32.770
And Fox, thanks very
much for your moderation

02:01:32.770 --> 02:01:36.310
of the panel, because I
think your work really has

02:01:36.310 --> 02:01:38.358
encompasses so
many of the themes

02:01:38.358 --> 02:01:40.150
that we've been seeing
here, and has been I

02:01:40.150 --> 02:01:44.380
think inspirational to a lot
of people, including myself.

02:01:44.380 --> 02:01:47.710
And I think what
this panel embodies

02:01:47.710 --> 02:01:51.670
in microcosm is certainly
the type of analysis

02:01:51.670 --> 02:01:54.580
which I think on the
computational side

02:01:54.580 --> 02:01:58.360
that I think people
have been increasingly

02:01:58.360 --> 02:02:02.320
aspiring to, to actually bring
the mathematical formalism

02:02:02.320 --> 02:02:06.408
in the models, into a
closer conversation,

02:02:06.408 --> 02:02:08.200
both with the arts and
the social sciences.

02:02:08.200 --> 02:02:12.670
And to understand how it
can inform that discussion

02:02:12.670 --> 02:02:17.365
without as Ruha said overly
dominating that discussion.

02:02:17.365 --> 02:02:17.990
Thanks so much.

02:02:17.990 --> 02:02:19.240
It's been a cultural humility.

02:02:19.240 --> 02:02:22.040
You made me feel very
humble in your remarks.

02:02:22.040 --> 02:02:24.220
To you as well as
appreciating your insights.

02:02:24.220 --> 02:02:27.170
So Behnaz?

02:02:27.170 --> 02:02:30.140
Can you also share
a final remark?

02:02:30.140 --> 02:02:31.110
Of course.

02:02:31.110 --> 02:02:36.500
And I just want to very
briefly echo Jon's comments.

02:02:36.500 --> 02:02:39.080
And I want to say
that also thank you,

02:02:39.080 --> 02:02:40.760
Fox, for organizing this panel.

02:02:40.760 --> 02:02:43.010
I've learned from all
the speakers here.

02:02:43.010 --> 02:02:48.240
I think the opportunities
for artists, and designers,

02:02:48.240 --> 02:02:51.030
and to learn from
other disciplines.

02:02:51.030 --> 02:02:56.780
But also have the sort of
transdisciplinary approach

02:02:56.780 --> 02:02:59.720
that can learn and borrow
from each discipline's, it's

02:02:59.720 --> 02:03:01.710
incredible in this
period of time.

02:03:01.710 --> 02:03:05.390
So I'm extremely excited
about the possibility

02:03:05.390 --> 02:03:07.280
of learning from
other disciplines,

02:03:07.280 --> 02:03:11.180
and bringing those ideas
into my own discipline in art

02:03:11.180 --> 02:03:12.590
and design.

02:03:12.590 --> 02:03:16.550
So thank you so much for
creating and fostering

02:03:16.550 --> 02:03:19.580
this platform.

02:03:19.580 --> 02:03:20.770
Well thank you so much.

02:03:20.770 --> 02:03:23.080
And I want to finally
just extend a heartfelt

02:03:23.080 --> 02:03:26.320
thanks to Ruha, Jon
and Behnaz for agreeing

02:03:26.320 --> 02:03:28.090
to join this panel.

02:03:28.090 --> 02:03:31.030
I've learned so much and been
so inspired by all of the things

02:03:31.030 --> 02:03:31.960
that you have said.

02:03:31.960 --> 02:03:36.280
And also thanking the
co-conveners of the symposium.

02:03:36.280 --> 02:03:43.900
And those who came up with the
idea of Unfolding Intelligence

02:03:43.900 --> 02:03:49.210
as convening maybe a year or
more ago, and to see it finally

02:03:49.210 --> 02:03:53.020
come to fruition in this
time of great reckoning we

02:03:53.020 --> 02:03:54.790
find ourselves in right now.

02:03:54.790 --> 02:03:59.770
So it's just an astounding, and
impressive, and valuable feat

02:03:59.770 --> 02:04:01.220
to bring us all together here.

02:04:01.220 --> 02:04:02.380
So thank you very much.

02:04:02.380 --> 02:04:03.100
Thanks to our audience.

02:04:03.100 --> 02:04:03.600
Thank you.

02:04:03.600 --> 02:04:05.300
Thank you to our
audience as well.

02:04:05.300 --> 02:04:08.340
And have a great rest
of the symposium.

